window.ATELIER_COURS["1BknT0A6K_6JeHmeKYhdUPC-_mnOLk6tW"] = {"title":"Immersion","body":"Available on zenodo – DOI : 10.5281/zenodo.17251375\nMemorandum on Multimedia Applications & Virtual Reality\nImmersion\nAuthor: Abdelkrim Mebarki\nMaster’s Program in Computer Science – Artificial Intelligence & Applications\nDepartment of Computer Science – Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science\nUniversity of Science and Technology of Oran\nOctober 2, 2025\n1. Immersion\nImmersion is a transfer (shift) of perception from physical to virtual world.\nImmersion = User + Device + Attractive Scenario + Digital 3D Creation\nEach component from this equation plays a slightly different role:\n- The user → He brings attention, perception, prior knowledge, and willingness to engage;\n- The device → It enables sensory input/output (VR headset, AR glasses, haptic devices, etc.);\n- The attractive scenario (Narrative/Context) → It provides meaning and motivation to stay \nengaged;\n- The digital 3D creation (Environment/Assets) → It provides visual, auditory, and interactive world.\nThe immersion state is not the sum of these factors, but the synergy among them:\n➔ If the user is distracted, immersion breaks, even with high-quality tech.\n➔ If the device is poor, presence is reduced, even if the scenario is rich.\n➔ If the scenario lacks engagement, the user may remain detached.\n➔ If the digital 3D world isn’t coherent or realistic, the illusion collapses.\nImmersion is the psychological state that emerges when a motivated user interacts with a digital\nenvironment through appropriate devices, within a coherent and engaging scenario.\nImmersion can be understood as the transfer of perception from the physical world to the virtual world. It \nis implemented through two complementary sides:\n1️⃣ Mental and emotional engagement: Which reinforces the user’s psychological adherence to the \nexperience. Mental engagement is built by cognitive challenge and meaningful tasks, while \nemotional engagement is built by narrative, aesthetics, and affective triggers. Together, they \nmake the VR world feel compelling and “real” to the mind.\n2️⃣ Sensory stimulation: It is achieved through high-quality hardware and precise 3D modeling that \ncreate a realistic perceptual environment. Sensory stimulation is obtained by delivering coherent, \nhigh-quality, and synchronized multisensory inputs. This tricks the brain into perceiving the virtual \nas real, complementing the mental and emotional engagement.\nThe combination of these two sides leads to a state of total immersion.\n2. Taxonomy of VR Interfaces\n1️⃣ Large Consumer Interfaces (mainstream, widely adopted)\n🔹Head-Mounted Displays (HMDs): Wearable display devices that cover the eyes, presenting \nstereoscopic 3D images and often integrating head-tracking sensors. Its functioning principle is as \nfollows:\n➔ Two small screens (or one split screen) project slightly different images to each eye → stereoscopy.\n➔ Gyroscopes, accelerometers, and external cameras track head orientation and movement.\n➔ The displayed image updates in real time according to the user’s gaze direction, producing a sense \nof presence.\nExamples: Meta Quest, HTC Vive, PSVR, Apple Vision Pro.\n\n🔹 VR Boxes / Mobile VR Shells: Low-cost headsets (like Google Cardboard) that use a smartphone as \nthe display and processor. Its functioning principle is as follows:\n➔ A smartphone screen displays split stereoscopic images.\n➔ Lenses inside the box focus the image for each eye.\n➔ Basic head tracking through the phone’s gyroscope and accelerometer.\nExamples: Google Cardboard, Samsung Gear VR.\n2️⃣ Niche Interfaces (specialized, high-cost, or limited adoption)\n🔹 Data Gloves (Haptic or Tracking Gloves): Wearable gloves equipped with sensors to capture hand/finger \nmovement and sometimes provide haptic or force feedback. Its functioning principle is as follows:\n➔ Tracking: flex sensors, IMUs, or magnetic sensors measure finger bending and hand orientation.\n➔ Feedback: vibrotactile motors or pneumatic/force systems provide sensations of touch or \nresistance.\n➔ Input is translated into gestures or manipulation of virtual objects.\nExamples: Manus VR, HaptX, SenseGlove.\n🔹 Immersive Desktops (Fish Tank VR, 3D monitors): Desktop-based stereoscopic displays that give a 3D \nimpression, sometimes combined with motion tracking. Its functioning principle is as follows:\n➔ A stereoscopic monitor (glasses-based or glasses-free) presents two slightly offset images.\n➔ Head/eye tracking adjusts perspective dynamically.\n➔ The user perceives a “window” into the virtual world.\nExamples: zSpace, early 3D CAD systems.\n🔹 CAVEs (Cave Automatic Virtual Environments): Room-sized systems with projection screens on multiple \nwalls, floor, and ceiling. Users wear stereoscopic glasses. Its functioning principle is as follows:\n➔ Projectors display synchronized stereoscopic images on walls/floor.\n➔ Tracking systems adjust images based on user position.\n➔ Multiple users can share the same immersive space, though usually one “drives” the perspective.\nExamples: CAVE2 (EVL, University of Illinois), large industrial visualization rooms.\n3️⃣ Auxiliary Interfaces (immersion enhancers, not standalone)\n🔹 Surround and 3D Sound Systems: Audio systems that reproduce spatialized sound, giving the \nimpression that sounds originate from specific directions in 3D space. Its functioning principle is as follows:\n➔ Binaural rendering simulates how sound waves reach each ear with slight timing/volume \ndifferences.\n➔ Ambisonics or surround systems reproduce sounds in 360° around the listener.\n➔ Coupled with head tracking, sounds shift dynamically as the user moves.\n🔹 Sensor Systems (Motion Detection and Tracking): Devices that capture the position, orientation, and \nmovement of the user’s body, head, hands, or eyes. Its functioning principle is as follows:\n➔ Optical tracking: cameras (e.g., inside-out tracking in HMDs, or external sensors like SteamVR \nbase stations) follow markers or natural features.\n➔ Inertial tracking: accelerometers and gyroscopes detect movement and orientation.\n➔ Hybrid systems: combine optical + inertial for accuracy.\n➔ Data is fed into the VR system to update the user’s perspective and interactions in real time.\nExamples: Oculus Insight (inside-out), SteamVR Lighthouse, eye-tracking modules.\n🔹 Haptic Accessories (optional but increasingly popular): Wearable or environmental devices that provide \ntouch, vibration, or force feedback to simulate physical sensations. Its functioning principle is as follows:\n➔ Vibrational motors, pneumatic bladders, or force-feedback actuators generate physical sensations.\n➔ Devices can simulate textures, impacts, resistance, or even full-body effects.\nExamples: bHaptics vest, Teslasuit, omnidirectional treadmills (Virtuix Omni).\n3. Behavior and meaning in VR\nIn virtual reality, interaction relies on a synchronized bidirectional communication between the user \nand the virtual environment. This interaction can be described through four complementary concepts:\n\n3.1. User-to-System Flow (Input)\n➔ The user generates behavioral input through actions, gestures, or decisions.\n➔ These inputs are captured by devices (e.g., sensors, controllers, gloves) and translated into \nmachine-readable commands.\n3.2. System-to-User Flow (Output)\n➔ The virtual environment delivers sensory output such as visual, auditory, and haptic data.\n➔ These outputs inform, guide, and influence the user’s perception and subsequent actions.\n3.3. Interpretation (Coding)\n➔ The user interprets the system’s outputs and adjusts behavior accordingly.\n➔ The system interprets the user’s inputs and updates the environment dynamically.\n➔ Meaning emerges through this mutual interpretation.\n3.4. Synchronized Communication\n➔ The exchange is not merely two-way but time-sensitive and coordinated:\n✓ Inputs and outputs must be processed and responded to in real time.\n✓ Any delay or desynchronization (e.g., latency, lag) disrupts immersion and presence.\n➔ Synchronization ensures that user actions and system feedback form a coherent loop.\n3.5. Communication Scenario (Organizing Framework)\n➔ This bidirectional synchronized communication is structured by a communication scenario:\n✓ A framework of rules of interaction (how actions are recognized and responded to).\n✓ A semantic layer (how signals, data, and feedback acquire meaning).\n✓ A set of goals or narratives (what the user seeks to achieve within the environment).\n➔ The scenario guarantees that both behavior and feedback contribute to an intelligible, meaningful, \nand immersive experience.\n4. Living in VR\nTransitioning between the real and virtual worlds involves cognitive adaptation processes, both upon \nentering a VR environment and upon returning to reality, which are essential to understanding immersion \nand presence:\nEntering a VR world (adaptation)\n➢ When users first enter a VR environment, they go through a short cognitive and perceptual \nadaptation period (seconds to minutes).\n➢ During this phase, they learn the rules of interaction and communication, understand the \nscenario’s logic, and align their sensorimotor expectations with the virtual environment.\nLiving in the VR world (immersion & presence)\n➢ Once these rules are understood, the user experiences immersion (the system’s technical \ncapacity to isolate the senses) and, more importantly, presence (the psychological feeling of \"being \nthere\").\n➢ At this point, cognition is effectively oriented toward the VR world, as attention, perception, and \ndecision-making processes adapt to the virtual context.\nLeaving the VR world (readaptation)\n➢ On exiting VR, the user often needs a readaptation period to recalibrate perception and cognition \nto the real environment.\n➢ This is related to aftereffects such as disorientation, perceptual lag, or reduced motor accuracy, \nwhich are well documented in VR studies.\nThe need for cognitive adaptation when entering and leaving VR reflects the depth of immersion and \npresence. If a user feels no difference between both transitions, it may indicate either a shallow level of \nimmersion, or conversely, an extremely seamless VR experience."};
window.ATELIER_COURS["19i-pFzocJ85Cc4NzOLtoD9Y3uwgCQkhG"] = {"title":"VR Concept","body":"Available on zenodo – DOI : 10.5281/zenodo.17203467\nMemorandum on Multimedia Applications & Virtual Reality\nVR Concept Definition\nAuthor: Abdelkrim Mebarki\nMaster’s Program in Computer Science – Artificial Intelligence & Applications\nDepartment of Computer Science – Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science\nUniversity of Science and Technology of Oran\nSeptember 25, 2025\n1. Computer Simulation\nA computer simulation is a virtual model that mimics how a real-world system/process behaves. Instead of \ntesting things in reality (can be expensive/risky/impossible) we use computer to experiment safely (efficiently).\nExamples\n- Scientists simulate weather patterns to predict storms.\n- Engineers simulate how a bridge will hold up before building it.\n- Doctors use simulations to train for surgeries.\nIt’s like creating a digital twin of something real, then watching how it reacts under different conditions. You \ncan tweak variables, test “what if” scenarios, and learn without touching the actual thing.\n1.1. Computer Simulation Pipeline\n➔ Define the System\n▪ Identify what you're simulating (e.g. traffic flow, weather, molecule behavior).\n▪ Set goals: What do you want to learn or predict?\n➔ Build the Model\n▪ Create a mathematical or logical representation of the system.\n▪ Choose variables, rules, and relationships.\n➔ Implement in Code\n▪ Translate the model into a computer program.\n▪ Use simulation software or custom code (e.g. Python, MATLAB, Unity).\n➔ Run the Simulation\n▪ Input initial conditions.\n▪ Let the system evolve over time or through iterations.\n➔ Analyze Results\n▪ Visualize outputs (graphs, animations, metrics).\n▪ Compare with real-world data or expectations.\n➔ Refine & Repeat\n▪ Adjust parameters or model structure.\n▪ Re-run to improve accuracy or explore new scenarios.\n2. What is VR ?\nVirtual reality is a computer-generated simulation that immerses the user in an artificial environment, \ndesigned to be immersive, interactive, and multimedia.\n2.1. Virtual Simulation\nThe virtual world is designed and animated using: 3D modeling, Animations and Real-time graphic engines.\n2.2. An Immersive Experience\nVR transfers the user’s perception from the real world to a virtual one. With headsets and sensors, it \nengulfs the user in a digital universe where the senses are stimulated.\n2.3. Real-Time Interaction\nInteraction is bidirectional and synchronized: The user acts on the virtual world, and it reacts instantly. To \nobtain interactivity, we need to define two scenarios:\n• Communication scenario – defines how the user interacts with the virtual world.\n• Content scenario – structures the actions, events, and responses of the virtual world.\n2.4. A Multimedia Experience\nVR engages multiple sensory channels to enhance immersion: Vision, Hearing and Touch (haptics)."};
window.ATELIER_COURS["15DOxfUM9LANVWhvbHWZUBpb0e5av-WMe"] = {"title":"Communication Multimedia","body":"Available on zenodo – DOI : 10.5281/zenodo.17203198\nMemorandum on Animated Multimedia Application Design\nCommunication and Multimedia\nAuthor: Abdelkrim Mebarki\nMaster’s Program in Computer Science – Artificial Intelligence & Applications\nDepartment of Computer Science – Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science\nUniversity of Science and Technology of Oran\nSeptember 25, 2025\n1. The Communication Process\n1.1. Two Main Actors: Sender and Receiver\nThe communication process is built around two primary actors: the Sender and the Receiver.\n• The Sender has an idea or intention that they want to share.\n• This idea must be encoded into a message that can be transmitted.\n• The Receiver receives the message and interprets it, transforming it into information.\nThe information reconstructed by the receiver can never correspond 100% to the sender’s original idea. \nInterpretation is always influenced by the receiver’s knowledge, context, and perception.\nExample: A teacher explains a concept in class (sender → message). Each student (receiver) understands \nit slightly differently depending on their background knowledge.\n1.2. Message and Physical Support\nFor a message to travel from sender to receiver, it needs a physical support. This support is the material \ncarrier of the message. It may be a screen (for digital text or video), a paper (for printed communication) pr \na a haptic vibrations on a device (such as a game controller).\nIt ensures that the abstract idea takes on a tangible form that can be perceived by human senses.\nExample: A love letter written on paper versus the same words sent in a text message—different supports, \nbut the same underlying intent.\n1.3. Forms of the Message\nThe content of the message can take various forms:\n• Text (a written paragraph, an email)\n• Speech (a spoken explanation, a voicemail)\n• Image (a photograph, a diagram)\nEach form is considered a channel when it is uniform, homogeneous, and continuous.\nExample: A news broadcast may combine multiple channels: a journalist’s voice (audio channel), text \ndisplayed on the screen (text channel), and supporting images (visual channel).\n1.4. Channels and Human Perceptual Dimensions\nEvery channel belongs to a broader dimension, which corresponds to a specific human perceptual domain:\n• Vision → text, images, videos.\n• Hearing → speech, music, sound effects.\n• Touch (Haptics) → vibrations, tactile feedback.\nCombining different channels across these dimensions makes the communication richer and more engaging.\nExample: In virtual reality, a game may use multiple dimensions: visual (3D graphics), auditory (sound \neffects, voice), and haptic (controller vibrations).\n✅ In summary:\n→ Communication begins with a sender’s idea, encoded into a message.\n→ The message travels via a support, in a particular form.\n→ The form defines the channel, which belongs to a human perceptual dimension.\n→ The receiver interprets the message to build information.\n→ The information will never be an exact replica of the sender’s idea.\n\n2. Media vs Multimedia\n2.1. Media, plurial of Medium (sense 1: means of communication)\nTechnical means used to transmit a message or information from a sender to a receiver.\nExample: speech, writing, an email.\n2.2. Media (sense 2: mass media)\nRefers to organized systems of mass communication, designed to reach a wide audience.\nExample: newspapers, radio, television, the Internet.\n2.3. Multimedia\nMultimedia refers to the combined use of several media or forms of communication (text, image, sound, \nvideo, animation, interactivity).\nExample: an educational website containing text, explanatory videos, interactive images, and audio.\n2.4. Multimedia application\nA digital application that uses several media to communicate with the user.\nA medium corresponds to a channel: a unique flow defined within a single perceptual dimension (such as \nsound, vision, or touch).\n3. Perception and perceptual Dimension\nPerception refers to the sensory experience of the internal world (self-perception) and the external world\n(perception of others).\nIt involves the five senses (touch, sight, hearing, smell, taste), as well as proprioception (the detection of \nbody movements) and bodily and emotional feeling (such as sensing cold, fear, or hunger).\nIt enables the identification and interpretation of both internal and external stimuli through cognitive functions \nsuch as memory.\nA perceptual dimension is defined as a unique and consistent stream of sensory input within the overall \nperceptual process.\n4. Hypermedia and Non-linear Navigation\nHypermedia is a system that connects multiple media through links, enabling non-linear navigation within a \nmultimedia application.\nNon-linear navigation is a non-sequential path through a multimedia application, made possible by links.\n4.1. Benefits of Non-Linear Navigation\n- User Control and Freedom\no Users can choose their own path through the content.\no Example: On an educational website, learners can jump directly to the topic they find most \nrelevant.\n- Personalized Learning/Experience\no Each user can adapt the navigation to their needs, interests, or pace.\no Example: In an e-learning platform, a student can skip basics and focus on advanced \nmodules.\n- Exploration and Engagement\no Encourages curiosity and active exploration rather than passive consumption.\no Example: Interactive museum kiosks where visitors explore artifacts via images, videos, and \nmaps.\n- Efficient Access to Information\no Users can reach specific information quickly without going through unnecessary steps.\no Example: Hyperlinked FAQs let you jump directly to the answer.\n4.2. Drawbacks of Non-Linear Navigation\n- Risk of Disorientation\n\no Too many links or pathways may confuse the user.\no Example: A website with excessive cross-links where users lose track of where they started.\n- Incomplete Understanding\no Skipping content may cause users to miss important steps or background knowledge.\no Example: In an online course, learners may jump to advanced topics without mastering the \nbasics.\n- Cognitive Overload\no Too much choice and too many navigation options can overwhelm the user.\no Example: A multimedia application with multiple menus, pop-ups, and links can distract from \nthe main message.\n- Design Complexity\no Developing a non-linear structure is harder for designers and requires careful planning.\no Example: Ensuring all hyperlinks are relevant, functional, and don’t create “dead ends.”\nIn Summary\nBenefits: Freedom, personalization, engagement, efficiency.\nDrawbacks: Disorientation, incomplete learning, overload, design complexity.\nThe key is balance: combining non-linear navigation with some structured guidance (like menus, maps, or \nrecommended paths) to support both freedom and clarity.\n5. Multimedia application architecture\nEvery multimedia application can be understood as a simulation, whether it represents a physical \nphenomenon or object, or a mental abstraction. In all cases, it is a digital representation of an existence, \neither concrete or abstract.\nThe process of creating a digital version of an idea, a phenomenon, or an object is called computer \nsimulation. It refers to the transition from physical reality (defined as a reality that requires no technological \ndevice to be perceived) to virtual reality (defined as a reality that requires interfaces to be perceived).\n5.1. The scene\nA multimedia application is composed of one or more scenes. Each scene is defined through perceptual \ndimensions and hypermedia links that enable navigation. The data of a scene are described by metadata, \nwhich specify both the format and the nature of the information. Finally, these data are interconnected by \nsemantic links, ensuring the fluidity and coherence of the scene.\nA scene can be defined as a temporal decomposition of multimedia content, defined by the discontinuities \nwithin the overall content flow.\n5.2. Semantic coherence\nIn the context of multimedia applications, semantic coherence means that all the different media elements \n(text, image, video, sound, animation, haptics, etc.) work together to convey a consistent meaning to the \nuser.\nSemantic coherence is the degree to which the various components of a multimedia application remain \nlogically and meaningfully aligned with the intended message, scenario, or concept.\n In practice\n• The text on a slide should match the images shown, rather than contradicting or confusing the user.\n• Background music should reinforce the mood of the scene (e.g., calm for learning, dynamic for \naction), not distract from it.\n• Animations should illustrate the same concept that the narration is explaining.\n Why it matters\nIf semantic coherence is broken (for example: cheerful music in a serious medical training simulation, or \nimages that contradict the spoken explanation), the user experiences cognitive dissonance or confusion, \nreducing comprehension and learning efficiency."};
window.ATELIER_COURS["1CmByNiB6J7vd-VLHoJHFcQDDIo6F1wSx"] = {"title":"Sound","body":"Available on zenodo – DOI : 10.5281/zenodo.18125385\nMemorandum on Multimedia Applications & Virtual Reality\nSound Immersion\nAuthor: Abdelkrim Mebarki\nMaster’s Program in Computer Science – Artificial Intelligence & Applications\nDepartment of Computer Science – Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science\nUniversity of Science and Technology of Oran\nJanuary 2, 2026\n1. Introduction\nSound is vibrations emitted by a physical source, reaching the human ear, transformed into neural signals \nand interpreted by the brain within a given auditory context, often called an acoustic configuration. This \nprocess allows us reconstructing a detailed “sound image” that conveys not only speech and emotions \nbut also spatial cues such as distance, direction, and environment.\n1.1. The acoustic configuration\nIt determines the final perception of sound including several key components:\nThe geometry of the scene –\nThis refers to the positions, distances, and dimensions of the sound sources in the real or the virtual \nenvironment. Their spatial arrangement directly shapes how sound waves propagate to the human ear.\nAcoustic and processing functions in the virtual world –\nThese functions simulate how sound is transformed when interacting with materials—such as absorption, \nreflection, diffraction, and occlusion—replicating the physical behavior of sound in real life.\nCapture and playback devices –\nIn the physical world, sound is captured by the human ear; in digital environments, playback is ensured \nthrough headphones or speakers, which influence the quality, spatial cues, and the immersion level.\n1.2. The interpretation\nOn the cognitive side, the interpretation refers to the mental reconstruction of the original scene based \nsolely on the auditory perception—even when some elements or objects are hidden. The human brain \ninfers distance, direction, movement, and even the nature of unseen objects through sound cues alone.\n👉 When auditory perception is combined with vision, the sense of presence is greatly enhanced, allowing \nthe body to feel fully immersed in the virtual world.\n1.3. HMI functions\nAnother important function of sound is its role in Human–Machine Interaction (HMI).\nSystem feedback sounds—such as clicks, alerts, confirmations, or ambient cues—accompany the user’s \nactions and increase usability, intuitiveness, and overall comfort.\n2. 3D Sound\nA 3D sound is a digitally processed audio signal that is designed to reproduce spatial cues allowing the \nlistener to reconstruct a full three-dimensional scene from what he hears. When properly played back—\ntypically through headphones or spatial audio speakers—This sound enables the user to perceive the \nposition, distance, and movement of each sound source within the environment. In other words, the listener \ncan localize where each sound is coming from as if it were occurring in the original real space.\n3. Surround Sound (Multichannel Audio)\nThe surround sound—also known as multichannel audio—is one of the earliest and most widely adopted \ntechniques for creating a 3D auditory experience. The principle is to distribute sound across multiple \nloudspeakers placed around the listener, thereby expanding the auditory field and enhancing immersion.\n3.1. Principle and Objective\nSurround sound relies on the idea that the sound can be spatialized by physically placing multiple \nloudspeakers at different angles and positions around the listener. Each speaker reproduces a distinct \naudio channel, allowing the auditory system to perceive directionality and movement through natural \ndifferences in –\n\n➔ Time of arrival (interaural time differences)\n➔ Intensity (interaural level differences)\n➔ Spectral filtering (head and torso effects)\n3.2. Typical Configurations\nSurround sound systems are commonly classified according to the number of channels and their \narrangement. Here are some examples –\n➔ 5.1 (Front Left, Front Right, Center, Surround Left, Surround Right, + subwoofer) – the most widely \nused in cinema and home theaters.\n➔ 7.1 (adds Rear Left and Rear Right for increased back coverage).\n➔ 9.1 or 11.1 (adds height channels for vertical dimension).\n👉 The first number indicates the number of full-range channels, while the “.1” refers to a Low-Frequency \nEffects (LFE) channel dedicated to bass.\n3.3. Rendering Process\nIn multichannel systems, spatialization occurs in two main stages:\na. Audio Encoding\nThe audio mix is created so that specific sounds are assigned to specific channels or are dynamically \npanned between the channels (e.g., moving car left to right is gradually shifted across multiple speakers).\nb. Playback / Rendering\nDuring playback, each audio channel is routed to its corresponding loudspeaker in the environment. The \nlistener’s perception of space emerges because the sound field surrounds them physically.\n3.4. Strengths\n➔ Natural and Robust Spatialization\nSpatial cues are produced physically by real speakers → Perception of directionality is strong and stable.\n➔ High Quality for Cinematic Experiences\nIt reproduces wide, dynamic scenes and convey movement, ambience and envelopment effectively.\n➔ Industry Standard\nFormats (Dolby Digital, DTS, THX) are adopted in film, broadcasting, home cinema, and gaming.\n3.5. Limitations\n➔ Requires Physical Speaker Placement\nAccurate spatialization depends on correct speaker positioning → Less flexible than other methods.\n➔ Limited Personalization\nDoes not adapt to head movements or individual ear shapes → Insufficient spatial accuracy for VR.\n➔ Not Suitable for Mobile or Head-Mounted Displays\nSurround sound requires a fixed listening position (“sweet spot”), however, in VR, users turn freely.\n3.6. Use Cases\n➔ Cinema and home theater systems;\n➔ Video games on consoles or PCs;\n➔ High-end presentation rooms and simulators;\n➔ Theme parks and multi-projector spaces.\n4. Principle of Loudspeaker Operation and Types of Speakers Drivers\n4.1. 1. Basic Principle of Loudspeaker Functioning\nA loudspeaker is a device that converts electrical audio signals into mechanical vibrations that \ngenerate sound waves in the air. This process relies on the interaction of three main components:\n1. Voice coil: A coil of wire that receives the electrical signal.\n2. Magnet: Creates a static magnetic field.\n3. Diaphragm (cone or dome): Moves back and forth to push air and produce sound.\nWhen the audio signal passes through the voice coil, it produces a varying magnetic field. This magnetic \nfield interacts with the permanent magnet, causing the coil—and therefore diaphragm—to move. This \nmovement displaces air at audible frequencies, creating the sound the listener perceives.\nBecause different frequencies require different types of movements and diaphragm sizes, no single \nspeaker driver is capable to reproduce the entire audible spectrum (20 Hz – 20 kHz) with high fidelity. This \nleads to the use of different specialized drivers and multi-way speaker systems.\n\n4.2. 2. Types of Speakers Drivers (According to Frequency Range)\nSubwoofers – Lowest Frequencies (∼20–120 Hz)\n➔ Size – Very large drivers\n➔ Function – Reproduce deep bass and sub-bass\n➔ Role – Often used to cover the lowest frequencies for the entire system\n➔ Why separate? Low frequencies require large diaphragm excursions and high power, which \nsmaller drivers cannot handle.\nWoofers – Low Frequencies (∼40–1000 Hz)\n➔ Size – Typically 10–38 cm in diameter\n➔ Function – Reproduce low to lower-mid frequencies\n➔ Limitations:\n✓ Perform poorly at high frequencies\n✓ Produce distortion when pushed into high-frequency ranges\n➔ Use – Essential in 2-way, 3-way, and 4-way systems\n➔ Woofers cover part of the midrange in simpler designs.\nMidrange Drivers – Middle Frequencies (∼250–5000 Hz)\n➔ Size – 8–17 cm\n➔ Function – Reproduce the core of human hearing: speech, instruments, and most detailed sounds\n➔ Advantage – Reduces strain on woofers and tweeters, improving clarity\n➔ Presence – Found mainly in 3-way and 4-way systems\nTweeters – High Frequencies (∼2000–20,000 Hz)\n➔ Size – Small dome or cone drivers, 1–5 cm in diameter\n➔ Function – Reproduce high frequencies (treble) such as articulation, sibilance, and brightness\n➔ Important constraint:\n✓ Cannot handle low frequencies\n✓ Sending bass or low midrange to a tweeter can cause mechanical damage\n4.3. Multi-Way Speaker Systems\nThe used speaker systems combine different drivers to cover the full frequency spectrum with accuracy.\nThese systems are classified according to the number of driver types they integrate:\n2-Way Systems\n➔ Drivers\n✓ Woofer (covers low + some midrange), Tweeter (high frequencies)\n➔ Advantages: Simplicity, low cost\n➔ Limitation: Less precise midrange reproduction\n3-Way Systems\n➔ Drivers – Woofer, Midrange, Tweeter\n➔ Advantages:\n✓ Better fidelity\n✓ Precise distribution of roles\n✓ Reduced distortion\n➔ Ideal for: Home audio, monitoring, cinema\n4-Way Systems\n➔ Drivers – Subwoofer, Woofer, Midrange, Tweeter\n➔ Advantages\n✓ Optimal coverage of all frequency bands\n✓ High clarity and dynamic range\n➔ Use: Professional audio, high-end installations\n4.4. 4. The Crossover Principle\nA crossover is an electronic filter that divides the audio signal into separate frequency bands and directs \neach band to the appropriate desired driver:\n➔ Low frequencies → Subwoofer / Woofer\n➔ Mid frequencies → Midrange driver\n➔ High frequencies → Tweeter\n\nCrossovers prevent:\n➔ Distortion (e.g., a woofer trying to reproduce high frequencies)\n➔ Damage (e.g., low frequencies reaching a tweeter)\nCrossovers may be:\n➔ Passive (integrated in the speaker)\n➔ Active (external electronic filtering before amplification)\nThey ensure that each driver operates in its optimal range, maximizing audio quality.\nDriver Type Frequency Range Size Role Notes\nSubwoofer 20–120 Hz Very large Deep bass System-wide low-frequency support\nWoofer 40–1000 Hz 10–38 cm Low/mid Distortion at high frequencies\nMidrange 250–5000 Hz 8–17 cm Speech + core details Improves clarity\nTweeter 2000–20,000 Hz 1–5 cm High frequencies Cannot handle bass\n5. Techniques for Reproducing Real 3D Sound\nReproducing a realistic three-dimensional auditory scene requires audio technologies designed to create \nspatial cues in all the perceived directions—horizontally, vertically, and with depth. Modern 3D audio \ntechniques go far beyond traditional stereo or surround sound by incorporating height channels, full\u0002sphere spatialization, and object-based rendering that adapts to listener movement.\n5.1. Reproducing Real 3D Sources with Loudspeakers (Hi-Fi Multidirectional Systems)\nTraditional high-fidelity (Hi-Fi) loudspeaker systems reproduce sound sources as accurately as possible \nwithin a room creating a wide stereo image or a horizontal surround field. However, to achieve a more \nimmersive 3D auditory experience, the system can be extended in two ways:\nAdding Height Information\nHeight cues improve vertical localization, enabling listeners to perceive sounds above and below them.\nTwo architectural strategies exist:\nThe first one – Ceiling Speakers\n➔ Physically installed above the listener.\n➔ Deliver direct height information.\n➔ Provide reliable vertical cues / Require complex room infrastructure.\n➔ Common in professional theaters, immersive rooms, and advanced home theaters.\nThe second one – Upward-Firing (Dolby Atmos) Modules\n➔ Loudspeakers angled upward to reflect sound off the ceiling.\n➔ The listener perceives the reflected wave as coming from above.\n➔ Easier to install / Less accurate than dedicated ceiling speakers.\n➔ Widely adopted in home Atmos systems.\nThis extends auditory field from 2D surround plane to 3D hemispherical or full-sphere configuration.\n5.2. Channel-Based 3D Audio\nChannel-based audio defines sound spatialization by assigning audio signals to specific loudspeaker \nchannels. The motion or direction perception are created by panning sounds between these channels.\n➔ Principle\nEach loudspeaker corresponds to a fixed direction in space. The listener perceives a sound’s position \ndepending on how the signal is distributed across these channels.\n➔ Advantages\n✓ Simple, stable, and compatible with existing speaker layouts.\n✓ Effective for cinema, home theater, and traditional VR caves (CAVEs).\n➔ Limitations\n▪ Not adaptive: the experience depends on the listener being in the “sweet spot”.\n▪ Does not natively encode vertical or continuous spatial information.\n▪ Lacks accuracy when the listener moves or rotates in VR.\nThis format is used in systems such as 5.1, 7.1, 9.1, and Atmos channel-based configurations.\n5.3. Ambisonics – Full-Sphere Spatial Audio\nAmbisonicsis a full-sphere audio format capable of reproducing sound from all the directions, including \nheight and depth components too. Ambisonics encodes the sound field itself, not the loudspeaker layout.\n\n➔ Principles\nAmbisonics represents the sound field using mathematical components:\n✓ Omnidirectional component (W)\n✓ Directional components (X, Y, Z)\nHigher-order ambisonics adds more components for increased spatial resolution.\n➔ Orders\nFirst-Order Ambisonics (FOA) – Lower precision; commonly used in consumer VR. (YouTube VR)\nHigher-Order Ambisonics (HOA) – Increases accuracy, spatial resolution, and realism (Facebook 360).\n➔ Advantages\n✓ Full-sphere representation.\n✓ Adaptable to any configuration of loudspeakers or headphones.\n✓ Highly suitable for 3D video, 360° VR environments, and immersive rooms (CAVEs).\n➔ Limitations\n✓ Requires decoding and potentially large numbers of speakers to get optimal results.\n✓ Capturing high-order ambisonics requires specialized non-standard microphones.\nAmbisonics represents one of the most flexible and powerful 3D audio formats in VR.\n5.4. Object-Based Audio\nObject-based audio represents a major evolution from channel-based systems in which, instead of \nassigning sound to channels, each sound is treated as an audio object with metadata that describes: its \nposition in 3D space, trajectory and movement, and its acoustic characteristics.\nDuring playback, the rendering engine calculates how each object should be reproduced depending on:\n✓ Listener orientation\n✓ Speaker configuration\n✓ Acoustic simulation (room, materials)\n✓ Device type (headphones, multichannel speakers, VR headset)\n➔ Advantages\n✓ Extremely flexible – adapts to any playback system.\n✓ High precision, ideal for VR and interactive environments.\n✓ Supports dynamic scenes where sound sources move or the listener rotates.\n➔ Examples\n✓ Dolby Atmos (object + channel hybrid)\n✓ DTS:X\n✓ Game audio engines (Unity Spatializer, Oculus Audio, Steam Audio)\n6. Methods of Sound Recording: Mono, Stereo, and Binaural\nSound recording techniques determine the amount of spatial information preserved when capturing an \nacoustic scene. While all methods store the main part of the audio information, they differ significantly in \ntheir ability to reproduce the corresponding realism, depth, and spatial localization. The three fundamental \napproaches are Mono, Stereo, and Binaural recording, each serving specific goals and constraints:\n6.1. Mono Recording\nMono (monophonic) recording uses single microphone to capture sound. Regardless of the number \nof output channels (even if exported as “stereo”), the signal originates from one single point. If a mono \nsignal is played through two output channels (left and right):\n➔ The same waveform is sent to both the speakers.\n➔ No time delay between channels.\n➔ No amplitude difference between channels.\nMono lacks spatial cues needed for realistic auditory localization because:\n➔ Only one channel of information exists.\n➔ There is no interaural time difference (ITD): sound reaches both ears at exactly the same time.\n➔ There is no interaural level difference (ILD): both listener ears receive the same intensity.\n➔ There is no directional filtering (HRTF) encoded.\nAs a result, the listener perceives the sound as coming from a centered, undefined position. Mono \nrecordings are therefore not spatial at all but are useful for –\n➔ Voice recording (podcasts, telephony)\n\n➔ Archival purposes\n➔ Environments where bandwidth is limited\n➔ Applications where spatial realism is unnecessary\n6.2. Stereo Recording\nStereo (two-channel) recording recreate a horizontal sound image by using two microphones placed in \na specific configuration allowing the listener to perceive width, direction, and depth on a horizontal plane.\nStereo technique uses two channels (Left and Right) carrying similar but not identical signals.\nLocalization relies on differences between the channels:\n➔ Interaural Time Differences (ITD) – Slight time offset between the two microphones\n➔ Interaural Level Differences (ILD) - One microphone receives louder signal depending on direction\nTwo major techniques are commonly used to record stereo sounds:\nXY Technique (Coincident Pair)\n➔ Two directional microphones placed at the same point with an angle between them.\n➔ Ideal for indoor and static sources.\n➔ Minimal phase problems because capsules are nearly coincident.\n➔ Creates a clean, stable stereo image.\nORTF Technique (Near-Coincident Pair)\n➔ Microphones separated by about 17 cm, angled at 110°.\n➔ Mimics human ear spacing.\n➔ Better for outdoor, dynamic, or naturalistic recordings.\n➔ Produces wider and more realistic stereo images.\nWhat Is a “Stereo Sound Image”?\nIt is the virtual horizontal space perceived between two speakers or headphones including:\n➔ Left–right positioning of sources\n➔ Apparent width of instruments or environment\n➔ Depth resulting from relative loudness and reverberation\nStereo does not reproduce height/full 3D space but provides convincing two-dimensional auditory scene.\n6.3. Binaural Recording\nThis recording aims to reproduce sound exactly as a human hears it, using dummy head or miniature \nmicrophones inserted into a listener’s ears capturing all the acoustic cues that allow spatial localization.\nThe core concept is Head-Related Transfer Function – HRTF (Anatomical Transfer Function – ATF).\nHead-Related Transfer Function (HRTF)\nHRTF describes how sound is filtered by the anatomy of the listener before reaching eardrum including:\n➔ Shape and size of the head\n➔ Structure of the outer ear (pinna)\n➔ Geometry of the ear canal\n➔ Nasal and oral cavities\n➔ Tissue density and skull reflections\nEach of above elements boosts some frequencies and attenuates others, depending on:\n➔ The direction of the sound source\n➔ The distance of the source\n➔ Whether the source is above, behind, or below the listener\nThis filtering provides critical cues for 3D localization.\nWhat Binaural Recording Enables\nWhen replayed through headphones, binaural recordings can reproduce:\n➔ Full 3D space (front, back, above, below)\n➔ Distance perception\n➔ Elevation cues\n➔ Precise horizontal and vertical localization\n➔ Occlusion and filtering by head and torso\n➔ Immersive realism similar to natural listening\nBecause it encodes the ear’s natural acoustic modifications, binaural audio is considered ideal for:\n➔ Virtual Reality (VR) and Augmented Reality (AR)\n➔ 3D audio simulations\n\n➔ Ambisonics decoding\n➔ Spatialized game audio\n➔ Research on perception and auditory cognition\nLimitations\n➔ Best experienced with headphones (loudspeakers reduce accuracy)\n➔ HRTF varies across individuals—generic HRTFs may not perfectly match every listener.\n7. Sound Rendering Pipeline in VR\nIn VR environments, sound rendering follows a structured pipeline that transforms raw the audio signals \ninto a final spatially coherent and immersive auditory experiences. This pipeline ensures that each sound \nsource is correctly generated, positioned, processed, and rendered according to the listener’s position and \nthe acoustic properties of the virtual scene content:\n7.1. Generation\nEach sound is generated / associated with a specific object, treated as isolated from the environment.\nThis step defines the source audio signal before spatial or environmental effects are applied.\n7.2. Association\nThe generated sound is then linked to the object’s movement and position within the virtual scene. At \nthis stage, distance, relative orientation, and environmental properties (room acoustics, absorption, \nand reflections) are considered to model how the sound propagates over the scene.\n7.3. Convolution / Spatialization\nThe system calculates the acoustic transformations required for each sound source to reflect the \nenvironment accurately including:\n➔ Convolutions with impulse responses of virtual spaces\n➔ HRTF filtering for binaural rendering\n➔ Occlusion and reflection effects\n7.4. Instantiation / Playback\nFinally, the processed sound is instantiated in the scene, ready to be rendered through the playback \nsystem (the devices: headphones or speakers). This step ensures that the listener perceives the sound in \nthe predefined correct position, direction, and spatial context.\n8. 3D Sound Imaging\nThe 3D sound imaging process relies on a sequence of spatial analysis, signal processing, and rendering \nsteps that collectively ensure accurate and immersive auditory perception in the virtual environment:\n8.1. Spatial Analysis\nThe system computes line-of-sight information between the virtual listener and all sound sources in the \nscene to determine which sounds are directly audible, which are occluded, and the relative distances and \nangles for accurate the spatial rendering.\n8.2. 2. Sound Processing\nEach sound source is processed according to its position and environment using digital signal \nprocessing (DSP) techniques:\n➔ Delay – Simulating distance and propagation time\n➔ Filtering – Modeling the attenuation and the frequency-dependent absorption by the materials\n➔ Panning – Positioning sounds in the stereo or multichannel field\n➔ Reverb and reflections – Simulating environmental acoustics\nThese processes allow the listener to perceive both the direction and the distance, enhancing immersion.\n8.3. 3. Output Rendering\nThe processed audio is rendered for headphone playback, generating a stereo or binaural output\nadapting dynamically to the user’s position and orientation. In addition, using head tracking, the spatial \ncues are updated in real time, ensuring that sounds appear to originate from the correct locations."};
window.ATELIER_COURS["1Idm0l8tjfHz0UW8fHBSTw65yeRpa8IyG"] = {"title":"Haptic","body":"Available on zenodo – DOI : 10.5281/zenodo.18092969\nMemorandum on Multimedia Applications & Virtual Reality\nHaptic Immersion\nAuthor: Abdelkrim Mebarki\nMaster’s Program in Computer Science – Artificial Intelligence & Applications\nDepartment of Computer Science – Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science\nUniversity of Science and Technology of Oran\nDecember 30, 2025\n1. Introduction\nThe final cognitive stage in a VR environment capable of producing a strong feeling of immersion is full \ninteraction. This interaction primarily relies on the user’s perception of the virtual world. However, this \nperception is ultimately a fully created illusion, as it is mediated through technological interfaces—HMD, \ncontrollers or haptic devices—rather than through direct physical contact as in the physical world.\nPerception in VR can support static or passive exploration, where the user can observe the environment \nbut cannot alter its elements. It can also enable dynamic or interactive exploration, where the user \nmakes decisions based on the sensory data gathered from the system, such as visual, auditory, or haptic \ncues. The decisions typically involve selecting an option from a set of available choices.\nThe haptic dimension plays a dual and crucial role in the interaction process. First, it enriches the user’s \nperceptual experience by adding tactile, kinaesthetic, and force-feedback information to the collected \nsensory data. Second, it serves as an input/output channel through which user can implement and execute \ndecisions within the virtual world, reinforcing the sense of agency and enhancing overall immersion.\n1.1. Why Do We Use Haptics in Virtual Reality?\nHaptics in VR serve two primary purposes: locating the user’s body within the virtual space and \nenabling meaningful interaction with virtual entities.\nFor the spatial localization, the system should determine both the position of the user’s body (The virtual \nidentity)—typically captured through motion-tracking technologies—and the posture or configuration of \nthe user limbs, which may be inferred through computer vision techniques: Segmentation techniques and \nadvanced image-analysis algorithms.\nTo act upon virtual objects, the system relies on effort-based input mechanisms and haptic feedback \ndevices. These technologies allow users not only to manipulate virtual elements within but also to receive \ntactile or force-related responses that simulate real-world physical interactions. The resulting reciprocal \nloop of action and feedback significantly enhances the application realism, precision, and immersion.\n2. The Haptic Dimension: Terminology and Sensory Foundations\nIn this context, the term haptic refers to the human sense of touch in its broadest and large interpretation. \nUnlike vision or audition, haptic perception is not localized to a single sensory organ but emerges \nfrom the combined activity of multiple sensory systems distributed throughout the full body. These systems \nallow humans to perceive the contact, the movement, the force, the posture, and the physical interaction \nwith the environment. In the context of virtual reality and interactive systems, understanding the haptic \ndimension requires distinguishing between several closely related sensory sub-modalities.\n2.1. Proprioception\nProprioception is the sense enabling individuals to perceive position, orientation, and movement of their \nbody and limbs in the space, without relying on the visual input. It results from integration of sensory \ninformation originating from body muscles, tendons, joints, and skin. Proprioception also incorporates \nelements of kinesthetic and vestibular perception, making it essential for coordinated movement and bodily \nawareness. In virtual environments, proprioception plays a critical role in embodiment, allowing users to \nfeel that their virtual actions correspond to their physical movements making the immersion fully natural.\n2.2. Vestibular Sense\nThe vestibular sense in the human body is responsible for the perception of balance, head position, and \nmotion, including the acceleration and deceleration. This information is acquired through the vestibular \nsystem located in the inner ear, particularly the semi-circular canals and otolith organs. Although VR \nsystems cannot directly stimulate the vestibular head organs, they can indirectly influence vestibular \nperception through visual motion cues, synchronized movement, and motion platforms. The proper \nalignment between visual and vestibular information is crucial to maintaining the user comfort and to\npreventing motion sickness over experiences.\n\n2.3. Tactile Sense\nThe tactile sense pertains to the perceived sensations through skin resulting from physical contact. It is \nassociated with the perception of pressure and texture, mediated by mechanoreceptors embedded in the \nskin. In contrast, thermal sensations and nociceptive sensations (pain) are generally considered separate \ncomponents of cutaneous perception. In VR applications, tactile feedback is commonly simulated using \nvibration, pressure, or surface modulation to convey contact with virtual elements.\n2.4. Kinaesthesia\nKinaesthesia, often closely associated with proprioception, refers more specifically to the sensation of \nbody movement and limbs. It arises from sensory receptors located in muscles, tendons, and joints, \nproviding information about motion, effort, and resistance. Kinaesthetic perception is fundamental for \nuser tasks involving manipulation, navigation, and physical interaction with the artificial creation. In \nimmersive systems, kinaesthesia is frequently stimulated through force-based interfaces that resist, guide, \nor constrain the user movements.\n2.5. Cutaneous Sense\nThe cutaneous sense encompasses all the sensations originating from the skin, including pressure, \ntemperature, and pain representing the direct form of physical contact perception. While pressure-based \nstimulation is widely used in haptic interfaces, temperature and pain are less commonly simulated due to \ntechnical and ergonomic limitations and body safety considerations. Nevertheless, the cutaneous sense \nremains a core component of the VR haptic perception, contributing significantly to realism perception and \nmaterial recognition.\n2.6. Force Feedback\nForce feedback refers to the mechanical generation of forces applied to the user body stimulating the \nkinaesthetic and cutaneous sensory systems. Force feedback devices employ motors, actuators, or brakes \nto create resistance, weight, or constraint corresponding to interactions with artificially created objects.\nThis form of feedback is coupled with visual displays to ensure the consistency between what the user \nsees and what he physically feels. Force feedback is a key enabler of realistic interaction in virtual \nenvironments, particularly in simulation platforms, training, and rehabilitation applications.\n3. The Four Fundamental Modalities of Cutaneous Sensation\nThe human tactile perception detects four main types of stimulation, each one corresponding to a \ndifferent class of skin receptors. These modalities form the basis of all tactile sensations:\n3.1. Mechanical Stimulation\nIncluding any physical deformation of the skin –\n➔ Pressure\n➔ Indentation (touch)\n➔ Vibration\n➔ Stretching and shear\n➔ Texture perception\nTheses sensations are detected by mechanoreceptors (Merkel cells, Meissner corpuscles, Pacinian \ncorpuscles, and Ruffini endings)\n👉 Most haptic devices reproduce mechanical stimuli (vibration, pressure, deformation).\n3.2. Thermal Stimulation\nThis refers to sensations of Heat and Cold. Thermal changes are detected by thermoreceptors (warm \nand cold receptors in the skin).\n👉 Some haptic devices simulate temperature using:\n - Thermoelectric modules (Peltier elements)\n - Conductive heating or cooling surfaces\n - Phase-change materials\n3.3. Chemical Stimulation\nThe human skin can perceive Irritants, pH changes, and Certain molecules. Chemical stimulation is \nmostly related to pain, itch, and protective reflexes, not to ordinary touch.\n\n👉 Haptic devices do NOT use chemical stimulation, because: it is unsafe, slow, unpredictable, and \nuncomfortable. It exists mainly in medical or research contexts, not VR/AR.\n3.4. 4. Electrical Stimulation\nElectrical currents stimulate nerves in the skin, creating: artificial touch sensations, tingling, tapping, and \nproprioceptive illusions. This is called electrotactile stimulation.\n👉 Some modern haptic devices use electrotactile feedback, because it is: lightweight (no motors), fast,\nsilent.\nExamples – Teslasuit, NeoSensory Buzz band, Haply electrotactile arrays (research prototypes), \nUC Berkeley skin patches (electroadhesion-based haptics)\nModality Detected by Skin Used in Haptic Devices? Examples\nMechanical Yes ✔ Very common vibration motors, pneumatic actuators, soft robotics\nThermal Yes ✔ Rare but emerging Peltier modules, thermal haptics\nChemical Yes ✘ Not used (dangerous) medical tests only\nElectrical Yes ✔ Modern research & suits Teslasuit, electrotactile pads\n4. Effort-Based Input Mechanisms vs Haptic Feedback Devices\nIn VR, it is important to distinguish between effort-based input mechanisms and haptic feedback \ndevices, as they play complementary but opposite roles within the interaction loop. Together, they form a \nbidirectional interaction system. The first captures the user’s physical intentions, while the second conveys \nthe system’s response back to the user from the virtual world, enabling natural, intuitive, and immersive \ninteraction in virtual reality environments.\n4.1. Effort-Based Input Mechanisms (Input → System)\nThese mechanisms refer to how the user acts on the virtual environment capturing the effort or force\nthe user intentionally applies in order to perform an action. Their main purpose is to detect the user actions.\nThese types of devices measure user-generated forces, movements, or muscle activity that serve as \ninput channels to control or manipulate virtual objects. In short, they interpret what the user wants to do\ninside the VR world.\nExamples –\n➔ A VR controller sensing pressure on its trigger.\n➔ A force sensor measuring how hard the user pushes or pulls.\n➔ A data glove detecting finger flexion forces.\n➔ EMG-based interfaces reading muscle activation to infer effort.\n4.2. Haptic Feedback Devices (System → User)\nThey refer to how the virtual environment responds to the user inputs producing sensations that \nsimulate real physical interactions. Their purpose is to provide tactile or force sensations back to the user.\nThese devices generate tactile cues (vibration, texture, temperature) or kinaesthetic cues (resistance, \nforce, weight, torque) to increase realism in VR worlds by substituting or mimicking real-world physical \nresponses.\nExamples –\n➔ A controller that vibrates when hitting a virtual object.\n➔ A force-feedback joystick that resists movement.\n➔ A haptic glove simulating texture or pressure.\n➔ A VR exoskeleton arm applying torque to simulate weight.\nOne-Sentence Distinction – Effort-based input captures what the user is trying to do while haptic \nfeedback devices tell the user what the virtual world is “doing back.”\n5. Tactile Displays (Cutaneous Haptic Systems)\nTactile displays or cutaneous haptic systems, are designed to simulate the sensitivity of human skin by \nthe generation of surface-level sensations. Unlike force-feedback devices acting on muscles and joints, \ntactile displays stimulate skin receptors to convey information related to contact with virtual objects.\n5.1. Pressure-Based Stimulation\n\nThis technique is one of the most fundamental tactile cues used in cutaneous haptics enabling users to \nperceive contact, surface texture, and object compliance. By applying localized pressure to the human \nskin, tactile displays create the illusion of interacting with solid, soft, or deformable virtual surfaces.\n5.2. Vibration-Based Feedback\nVibration is widely employed to represent roughness, impacts, and surface irregularities of virtual entities. \nHigh-frequency vibrations are typically used to simulate fine textures, while short vibration impulses often \nsignal collisions or discrete interaction events within the artificial world.\n5.3. Thermal Stimulation\nThermal feedback announces sensations of heat and cold. It provides material-related cues associated \nwith different surfaces such as metal, ice, or warm objects. Even a minimal temperature variation can \nsignificantly enhance realism by reinforcing and complementing the perceived visual information.\n5.4. Physical Touch and Dynamic Contact\nTactile displays reproduce physical touch sensations such as objects pressing against the skin or moving \nacross it. It encompasses dynamic contact patterns supporting sensations like sliding, tapping, or brushing, \nthereby enriching the sense of interaction.\n5.5. Skin Stretch and Shear Feedback\nAdvanced tactile techniques involve skin stretch and shear stimulation. They generate directional cues \nrelated to friction, motion, or slip and are particularly effective for conveying movement across surfaces or \nsupporting precise object manipulation.\n5.6. Contactless and Electrical Tactile Technologies\nEmerging tactile technologies extend feedback beyond direct classical mechanical contact. Ultrasonic \nmid-air haptics use focused ultrasound to produce contactless tactile sensations in free space.\nElectrotactile stimulations apply controlled electrical impulses in order to simulate pressure or complex \ntextures without relying on mechanical actuators.\n5.7. Air-Based and Soft-Robotic Approaches\nAdditional tactile feedback techniques include the pneumatic or the air-jet systems, where pulsed air \nsimulates touch or airflow. Soft-robotic actuators deform to convey sensations of squishiness and \nelasticity allowing for more organic and compliant tactile interactions.\n5.8. Vibroacoustic and Resonance-Based Haptics\nFinally, vibroacoustic or resonance-based haptic systems rely on high-frequency vibrations to \nreproduce fine textures and sharp impacts enriching tactile interaction by adding subtle yet informative \nsurface cues in VR environments.\n6. Force-Feedback Devices (Kinesthetic Haptic Displays)\nForce-feedback devices or kinesthetic haptic displays, apply controlled mechanical forces to the user’s \nbody to simulate physical interaction with virtual entities. Unlike tactile displays that stimulate skin surface, \nthey act on muscles and joints, enabling users to perceive forces, resistance, and movement constraints.\n6.1. Resistance and Mechanical Properties\nOne of the main functions of force-feedback systems resisting user movements, thereby simulating \nphysical properties such as weight, inertia, stiffness, and mass. By opposing motion, these peripherals\ncreate the sensation that virtual objects possess substance and realistic mechanical behavior.\n6.2. Motion Constraints and Collision Simulation\nForce-feedback devices can constrain user motion too. This happens to reproduce collisions or the \npresence of solid surfaces. When a user encounters virtual boundaries or obstacles, the device limits the \nuser movements accordingly, reinforcing the illusion of contact with rigid or impenetrable objects.\n6.3. Directional Forces and Motion Guidance\nIn addition to resistance and constraints, force-feedback systems can apply directional forces guiding\nmotion or indicate properties of virtual objects. This guidance is particularly valuable in training and \nrehabilitation contexts, where forces can assist, correct, or deliberately resist the user movements in\ncontrolled and repeatable manner.\n6.4. Simulation of Deformable and Soft Objects\n\nAnother advanced force-feedback technology enables the simulation of deformable or soft virtual objects. \nSoft-robotic actuators, constructed from flexible materials such as silicone or electroactive polymers, \ndeform under interaction conveying sensations of elasticity, compliance, or squishiness, closely \napproximating the behavior of real-world soft materials.\n6.5. Shape-Memory Alloy–Based Actuation\nOther force-feedback approaches rely on shape-memory alloys (SMA). SMA change shape when they \nare electrically activated making it possible to generate localized or finger-level resistance. They are \nparticularly well suited for wearable configurations, including haptic gloves and soft exoskeletons.\n6.6. Exoskeletons and High-Force Actuation Systems\nExoskeleton-based systems extend force feedback to the fingers, hands, or arms, providing precise \nresistance and guided movement. In this context, pneumatic and hydraulic systems use fluid pressure to \napply larger forces and are often employed in full-body system configurations. Electromechanical linear \nactuators offer high-precision force or torque and are used in desktop-based or training simulators.\n6.7. Integrated and Hybrid Force-Feedback Platforms\nFinally, force-feedback technologies can be integrated into larger platforms such as haptic chairs or \nlocomotion systems to simulate a whole-body motion, impacts, or environmental effects. Hybrid devices \nenhance realism by combining kinesthetic force feedback with tactile stimulation, resulting in a more \ncomplete and convincing sensorimotor experience in VR.\n7. Design Constraints and Challenges in Haptic Systems\n7.1. System Bulk and Mechanical Complexity\nHaptic and force-feedback devices remain inherently cumbersome due to their mechanical complexity, \nphysical size, and overall weight. They often rely on rigid structures, multiple actuators, and external \nsupport frames to generate precise forces or tactile cues. While such an architecture enables high-fidelity \nfeedback, it can reduce portability and the ergonomic user comfort. As a result, integrating haptic \ntechnologies into everyday virtual or augmented reality applications requires a very careful balance \nbetween performance, ergonomics, and practical and technical deployment constraints.\n7.2. Installation Requirements and Safety Considerations\nThe installation of haptic systems introduces technical and safety challenges, particularly in relation to \nelectrical conductors, power delivery, and actuator behavior. Poor insulation, inadequate cable \nmanagement, or control system failures may expose users to real electrical risks or unintended mechanical \nforces. In order to mitigate these hazards, haptic systems have to adhere to a strict safety standard and \nincorporate fail-safe mechanisms, robust control logic, and real-time monitoring ensuring reliable and \nsecure operation during human interaction.\n7.3. Degree of Motion Freedom and Device Grounding\nThe degree of motion freedom provided by a haptic device impacts directly both realism and usability. \nGrounded systems, fixed to an external structure such as a desk or frame, generate high forces and \nprecise motion constraints but inherently limit the user mobility. In contrast, ungrounded or wearable \ndevices allow greater freedom of movement and support mobile or room-scale experiences, although this \ntypically comes at the cost of considerable reduced force output and stability. Choosing between grounded \nand ungrounded designs therefore involves a careful trade-off based on application requirements.\n7.4. Control of Stimulus Magnitude\nThe magnitude of haptic stimuli has a critical role in shaping the perceived intensity and realism of \ninteraction. Weak feedback may fail to convey meaningful physical properties, while excessive forces, \npressures, or vibrations can overwhelm the user or distort perception. Effective haptic system design \ndepends on precise control of stimulus amplitude to achieve the perceptual relevance without \ncompromising the user comfort or the system reliability.\n7.5. Modulation Within Human Tolerance Limits\nCrucially, all haptic stimuli must be modulated within the physiological and perceptual limits of the human \nbody. Muscles, joints, and sensory receptors possess well-defined tolerance thresholds beyond which \ndiscomfort, fatigue, or injury occur. Respecting these limits through well-defined and adaptive control \nstrategies, safety constraints, and user-specific calibration is very essential to ensure both physical safety \nand long-term usability, while still maintaining a convincing and immersive sensorimotor experience."};
window.ATELIER_COURS["1caPLcO6Hb4w0PQICmanBBDIMtfCTrB1L"] = {"title":"Vision","body":"Available on zenodo – DOI : 10.5281/zenodo.17918661\nMemorandum on Multimedia Applications & Virtual Reality\nVisual Immersion\nAuthor: Abdelkrim Mebarki\nMaster’s Program in Computer Science – Artificial Intelligence & Applications\nDepartment of Computer Science – Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science\nUniversity of Science and Technology of Oran\nDecember 12, 2025\n1. Introduction\nThe real world is perceived in two ways:\n1. Natural direct perception –We perceive our environment without any interface or device.\n2. Mediated (diffused / delayed) perception – It occurs through peripherals such as screens, \nprojectors, or headsets.\nMediated perception can represent both physical and virtual entities. The main goal of visual \nimmersion is to create virtual objects and environments convincingly stimulating the user’s visual system, \nmaking them naturally integrated into the user’s perception.\n1.1. Immersion of the Gaze\nImmersion of the gaze means that the user’s visual field is occupied only by the artificial environment.\nWhen the visual system is entirely surrounded by artificial imagery, the brain interprets it as a real and\ncontinuous space. This phenomenon reduces external visual references from the real world, strengthening \nthe feeling of presence in virtual world. The quality of gaze immersion depends on: Field of view (FOV) ; \nImage resolution ; Refresh rate and response latency.\nThe more natural and responsive the visual feedback, the stronger the sense of immersion.\n2. Total and Partial Immersion through Image Framing\nVisual immersion is classified according to how completely the visual space is filled by the virtual scene:\n2.1. Partial Immersion:\nOccurs when the user perceives the virtual content through a limited visual window (smartphone screen, \ncomputer monitor, or low-cost headset). The display frame remains visible, and the physical environment \ncontinues to influence user perception.\n2.2. Total Immersion:\nIs achieved when the virtual environment occupies the entire visual field, leaving no visible boundary \nbetween the image and the external world. This is typically obtained through Head-Mounted Displays or \nlarge-scale projection systems. Here, the user perceives only the virtual scene, resulting in a powerful \nillusion of presence and interaction within the digital created world.\n3. Conditions for Total Visual Immersion in Virtual Reality\nVisual immersion is reached when the user’s entire visual perception is dominated by the virtual scene, to \nthe point that real-world visual cues are either excluded or forgotten. To satisfy this state, several technical \nand perceptual conditions have to be fulfilled:\n3.1. Full Coverage of the Visual Field (Field of View – FOV)\nThe display occupy almost all the user’s natural field of vision — horizontally & vertically. A wide FOV \n(>100°) eliminates peripheral references to the real world enhancing the sense of presence.\n3.2. High Visual Quality (Resolution and Refresh Rate)\nHigh resolution and high refresh rate maintain stable and convincing visual experience:\n➔ High resolution ensures sharp, detailed images and prevents pixelation effect.\n➔ High refresh rate (>90 Hz) guarantees smooth motion and reduces flicker and motion sickness.\n\n3.3. Low Visual Latency and Real-Time Feedback\nAny delay between head/eye movement and the corresponding update of images must be imperceptible \n(<20 ms). Low latency preserves spatial coherence —world moving with user’s gaze, reinforcing realism.\n3.4. Stereoscopic Vision and Depth Cues\nEach eye receives a slightly different image producing binocular disparity, the natural mechanism of depth \nperception. Combined with motion parallax (view changes as the user moves), this creates 3D illusion.\n3.5. Correct Perspective & Natural Scaling\nObjects in the virtual scene must respect real-world proportions and perspective rules. Anomalies in scale \nor distance perception can instantly break immersion level and reduce scenario credibility.\n3.6. Precise Head and Eye Tracking\nThe visual system must dynamically react to user’s gaze and head orientation. The accurate tracking \nallows adjustment of natural viewpoint and maintaining alignment between the vision and the behavior.\n3.7. Isolation from External Visual Stimuli\nThe visual system must be shielded from all real-world distractions (light, room edges, visible devices).\nHead-Mounted Displays and immersive rooms achieve this by enclosing user in fully visual environment.\n3.8. Consistency and Coherence of the Virtual Scene\nThe artificial world has to remain visually stable, obeying physical and spatial rules as the real world (such \nas lighting and perspective). Visual incoherence or graphical artifacts instantly disrupt the illusion.\n✅ In summary – Total visual immersion → User’s field of vision entirely filled with coherent, dynamic, \nand realistic virtual imagery that reacts naturally to their movements, excluding real-world visual entities.\n4. Human Depth Perception – How It Works\nThe ability to perceive distances and 3D structures is multi-factorial, not based only on a single mechanism.\nIt comes from stereoscopic vision, but monocular cues contribute too.\n4.1. Binocular (Stereoscopic) Vision – The Primary Mechanism\n➔ Each eye sees the world from a slightly different angle → Binocular Disparity.\n➔ The brain merges these two images to reconstruct the depth and the volume.\n➔ This mechanism is very strong at both short and medium distances (~20 meters).\n➔ It’s the foundation of 3D perception in VR — Headsets display two slightly offset images (one per eye).\n4.2. Monocular Cues – Complementary Depth Information\nEven with one eye, the brain can still infer depth using several cues:\nType Description Example\nPerspective Parallel lines converge in the distance A road narrowing toward the horizon\nSize Familiar objects appear smaller when farther A distant person looks smaller\nOcclusion One object hides part of another A tree blocking a building\nShadows & Light Lighting gives clues about shape and position A sphere lit from one side looks 3D\nMotion Parallax Near objects move faster across the field of view Seen while moving or turning the head\nTexture Gradient Distant textures appear smoother Grass looks detailed nearby, blurry far away\nThese cues simulate depth even on a flat screen or photograph.\n4.3. Image Resolution – Only an Indirect Factor\n➔ High resolution improves the clarity and precision of depth cues (texture gradients, edges, contrast).\n➔ It does not generate depth by itself — Only helps the brain interpret depth information more accurately.\n➔ We can have high-resolution flat image that still looks 2D if it lacks binocular or monocular depth cues.\nMechanism Type Role in Depth Perception\nStereoscopic Vision Binocular Main physiological source of real 3D depth\nMonocular Cues Cognitive/Visual Complementary, especially at long distances\nHigh Resolution Technical Enhances clarity of depth cues, but not a depth mechanism itself\n\n4.4. Can a person with only one eye perceive depth?\n Yes — but differently and less precisely. A person with only one eye vision (monocular vision) can\nstill perceive depth and estimate distances relying entirely on monocular depth cues.\n1. What is lost:\n➔ The brain no longer receives two different images to compare (no binocular disparity).\n➔ As a result, true stereoscopic depth at short range disappears.\n➔ Tasks requiring fine distance judgment (e.g., catching a ball, threading a needle) become harder.\n2. What remains:\nThe brain uses monocular depth cues to reconstruct a sense of depth and distance:\n➔ Perspective – Parallel lines converge in the distance.\n➔ Relative size – Familiar objects appear smaller when farther away.\n➔ Occlusion – One object covering another indicates it’s closer.\n➔ Shading & light – Shadows reveal shape and depth.\n➔ Motion parallax – Moving your head, nearer objects shift faster across your FoV than distant ones.\n Over time, people with one eye become skilled at using these cues. Their brain adapts and learns to \nestimate distances cognitively rather than physiologically.\n3. Practical impact:\n➔ Static depth (when not moving) is less accurate.\n➔ Dynamic depth (while moving) can be quite effective thanks to motion parallax.\n➔ Everyday activities are manageable after adaptation, but fine depth precision remains limited.\n✅ In summary – A person with one eye can perceive depth, but through visual interpretation (monocular \ncues and experience), not through true stereoscopic fusion.\n4.5. What Is Monocular Motion Parallax?\nMotion parallax is a depth perception mechanism that uses the relative motion between the objects and \nthe observer to estimate the distances. When someone moves his head or body, objects at different \ndistances appear to move at different speeds and directions across his retina:\n➔ Nearby objects seem to move quickly and in the opposite direction of his motion.\n➔ Distant objects move slowly and often in the same direction as him.\nHis brain analyzes these differences to compute spatial depth, even when using only one eye.\n👁️ Example – Imagine you’re sitting in a moving car:\n➔ Trees near the road seem to race past you 🌳💨\n➔ Mountains far away barely seem to move at all ⛰️ Your brain interprets this as: “Trees are close, \nmountains are far”.\n➔ Even with one eye closed, this depth sense works — because it’s based on motion, not stereoscopy.\n⚙️ How It Works (Perceptual Mechanism)\n1. Visual perspective changes slightly over time – each moment provides a new image of the scene.\n2. The brain compares these temporal changes (like frames in a video).\n3. From each object’s apparent motion Speed and Direction, the brain deduces their relative distances.\nThis process is called temporal disparity, as opposed to the spatial disparity used in binocular vision.\n🎮 In Virtual Reality and 3D Applications\nMotion parallax reinforces depth perception, even when stereoscopy is limited or absent. For example:\n➔ Head tracking in VR (via sensors) allows the image to shift dynamically as the user moves.\n➔ This creates realistic motion-based depth, similar to what the human brain expects in real life.\n➔ Monoscopic VR (One image VR mobile) – parallax motion creates convincing illusion of depth.\nMechanism Type Requires 2 Eyes? Works in VR? Depth Range Key Principle\nStereoscopic Vision Binocular ✅ Yes ✅ Yes Short–medium Spatial Disparity (2 views)\nMotion Parallax Monocular ❌ No ✅ Yes Medium–long Temporal Disparity (motion-based)\n\n✅ In summary – Motion parallax → Perception of depth → Relative motion between Observer and \nobjects → Dynamic monocular cue → Brain understands 3D from movements, not from separate images.\n4.6. 🧠 Is Motion Parallax a Simulation of Binocular Vision?\nMotion parallax can be seen as a temporal substitute for spatial binocular vision. But it’s not identical.\n👁️ In Binocular Vision\n➔ Two eyes separated horizontally (~6 cm).\n➔ Each eye captures the world from a slightly different spatial viewpoint at the same time.\n➔ The brain instantly compares two simultaneous perspectives (Spatial Disparity) to compute depth.\n🌀 In Motion Parallax (Monocular Vision)\n➔ Only one eye, but moving slightly as the head is shifted.\n➔ At time t₁, the eye sees the scene from one position.\n➔ At time t₂, the same eye sees it from a slightly shifted position.\n➔ The brain compares these two successive images (Temporal Disparity) to estimate depth.\n🔍 Simulated Binocular Vision\nThe moving eye in motion parallax acts like two eyes, one before and the other after movement. The \nbrain compares the difference between two “snapshots” like it compares images from left and right.\nHowever:\n➔ In stereoscopy, images are captured simultaneously → Perfect for fast depth calculation.\n➔ In motion parallax, the brain must rely on memory and temporal integration of what it just saw — it’s \nslightly slower and less precise.\nMotion parallax is not true stereoscopy, but a functional analog of it.\nFeature Binocular Vision Motion Parallax\nType Spatial stereopsis Temporal stereopsis\nSource Two eyes (left/right) One eye (moving over time)\nTiming Simultaneous Sequential\nPrecision Very high (short range) Medium (dynamic scenes)\nIn VR True 3D Simulated 3D via head tracking\n💡 Simplified Analogy\nBinocular vision = two eyes at once\nMotion parallax = one moving eye over time\nBoth give the brain two perspectives — one in space, one in time — allowing it to reconstruct depth.\n5. Visual Immersion Interfaces in Virtual Reality\nVisual immersion is the primary key of virtual reality (VR). It refers to the user’s ability to feel visually \nsurrounded by the synthetic environment, perceiving objects as if they were truly present in his native \nspace. This illusion is produced by a combination of optical, electronic, and perceptual mechanisms, \nimplemented through a variety of peripherals and display technologies. These immersive interfaces are\ngrouped into three main categories: fixed systems, wearable systems, and emerging technologies.\n5.1. Fixed (Stationary) Immersive Interfaces\nImmersive Rooms (CAVEs)\nA CAVE (Cave Automatic Virtual Environment) is a room-sized cube where \nimages are projected on several walls, and sometimes on the floor and \nceiling. The user stands or moves inside the room space wearing stereo \nglasses tracked by motion sensors. Projection of stereoscopic synchronized \nimages on each surface creates a powerful sense of spatial presence.\nCAVEs are used for scientific visualization, design review, and collaborative \nVR because they allow several users to share the same immersive scene.\n\nImmersive Desks and Workbenches\nSmaller projection environment — Horizontal or inclined surface where 3D objects\nappear in depth. The user views the virtual objects through stereoscopic glasses, \nsometimes combined with head tracking and haptic devices offering semi-immersive \nvisualization suitable for medical imaging, industrial design, and education.\nLarge Displays and Projection Screens\nThis system uses one or more large high-resolution screens or curved \nprojectors to surround the main frontal part of the user’s visual field.\nAlthough not fully immersive, they provide a wide field of view and are \nused in flight simulators, training systems, and panoramic installations.\nStereoscopic Displays\nStereoscopic displays present two slightly different images to the left and \nright eyes. The brain fusions them to form a 3D perception. These displays \ncan be flat monitors, projectors, or even television screens relying on optical \nseparation methods to deliver different images to each eye.\n➔ Parallax Barrier Displays (Obsolete)\nA parallax barrier display uses a layer of slits placed in front of the screen to \ndirect different pixels toward each eye. This allows depth perception without \nglasses (called autostereoscopic). However, the constrained narrow \nviewing angle and brightness loss make it unsuitable for immersive VR →\nMainly used in small portable 3D displays or research prototypes.\n➔ Lenticular Displays\nSimilar to the parallax barrier, lenticular lenses refract light in different directions, sending one distinct \nimage to each eye. This device is autostereoscopic too creates a 3D impression without eyewear but \nwith limited accuracy. Lenticular technology is still used in advertising panels and handheld devices, but \nrarely in high-end VR systems.\n➔ Glasses-Level Separation Techniques\nThese interfaces rely on optical or electronic filtering to send different images from the screen to each \neye, producing stereoscopic depth.\n➔ Colorimetric Differentiation (Anaglyph Glasses) – Obsolete\nAn anaglyph device encodes left and right images in complementary colors (usually red and cyan).\nEach glasses lens filters one color, allowing each eye to see its corresponding image. Although it offers \na basic 3D experience, it distorts colors and causes eye fatigue.\n➔ Polarizing Glasses (Passive)\nThese systems use light waves oscillating in different planes. The projectors emit light polarized \ndifferently, and each lens passes only one orientation. This spatial multiplexing (two projectors side-by\u0002side) or temporal multiplexing (alternating frames) induces stereoscopic vision. Polarized projections are \nused in cinema and large-screen VR installations, as it allows several users to share the same content.\n➔ Active Shutter Glasses (Electronic)\nActive glasses are based on liquid crystal shutters that alternately block each eye vision in \nsynchronization with the screen refresh rate. Each eye receives their images in sequence, producing \nfull-color stereoscopic view. This technique was dominant in early 3D computer graphics and simulations.\n5.2. Mobile and Wearable Interfaces\nHead-Mounted Displays (HMDs)\nIt is the most common immersive interface in modern VR integrating stereoscopic \ndisplays, motion tracking, and head orientation sensors into a headset worn by the \nuser. Rendering images according to head movement allows the HMD providing a \nreal-time egocentric experience.\n\nSmart Glasses and Augmented Reality Headsets\nSmart or AR glasses mix virtual content with real environment using semi-transparent \nlenses or waveguides to overlay information onto the physical world. These devices \nenable mixed reality, where users can interact with both real and virtual elements \nsimultaneously.\nSmartphone-Based Viewers (VR Boxes, Cardboard) – Obsolete\nEarly mobile VR emulators used smartphones inserted into plastic/cardboard headsets\ndisplaying two side-by-side images forming a simple stereoscopic view. These systems \nprovided low-cost access to VR lacking positional tracking and interactivity.\n5.3. Emerging and Hybrid Interfaces\nLight Field Displays\nLight field technology reproduces the direction and intensity of light rays in space. This approach enables\nperception of natural depth and changes of focus without stereoscopy technics.\nHolographic Displays\nHolography reconstructs the complete light wavefront of 3D models, allowing true volumetric perception \nwithout glasses. This is currently used in research labs and prototype installations.\nPanoramic Dome Projections\nPanoramic Dome Projection Systems (Fulldome or Immersive Dome Systems) are large hemispherical \nprojection environments that surround the viewer with imagery on a curved surface, (180° or even 360°).\nProjection principle:\n5 to 20 projectors are precisely calibrated to cover the entire dome. The \nprojected images are blended together to form one continuous \npanoramic scene. A single fisheye projector can be used at the \ncenter to project the entire dome view.\nImmersion mechanism:\nThe visual field is completely filled (from the floor to the ceiling) \n→ The viewer feels inside the environment rather than watching \nfrom outside stimulating peripheral vision, enhancing immersion \nand spatial awareness.\nApplications:\n➔ Museums and planetariums → Astronomy, natural history, and\neducational 360° experiences.\n➔ Simulation rooms → Training for aviation, driving, military, or urban planning.\n➔ Collaborative VR installations → Multiple users sharing the same environment without wearing HMDs.\nInteraction:\nSome domes integrate motion tracking or gesture interfaces, enabling limited interactivity.\nStatus:\nWidely used in scientific visualization and cultural installations, though not personal VR: It’s a shared, \nlarge-scale immersive installation rather than an individual headset.\n6. Conclusion\nFrom early anaglyph glasses to modern light field headsets, visual immersion interfaces have evolved \nfrom optical illusions to fully interactive installations. Technologies represent a compromise between cost, \ncomfort, resolution, and immersion. HMDs dominate personal VR experiences, while CAVEs and domes \npersist in shared and professional contexts. Future systems will increasingly aim for removing devices \nwhile maintaining natural 3D vision, combining the best of optical science and perceptual psychology."};
window.ATELIER_COURS["1XhY9s24RP0ZVGPsaczfs1durjcHt0cfq"] = {"title":"Modeling VRW","body":"Available on zenodo – DOI : 10.5281/zenodo.17627209\nMemorandum on Multimedia Applications & Virtual Reality\nModeling VR Worlds\nAuthor: Abdelkrim Mebarki\nMaster’s Program in Computer Science – Artificial Intelligence & Applications\nDepartment of Computer Science – Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science\nUniversity of Science and Technology of Oran\nNovember 16, 2025\nTo perceive a virtual world, the user needs entities to be rendered on devices. But before rendering can \nhappen, that world must first exist in a digital form — as a coherent structured representation of all \nits objects, spaces, and interactions.\n What does this mean?\n➔ The virtual world the user sees, hears, or feels does not exist physically (No external existence).\n➔ It must be created, described, and encoded in a form the computer can understand and process.\n➔ This internal digital description is what we call the VR World Representation or VR Modeling.\nTo create a Virtual Reality (VR), we must first model the virtual world — that is, design and implement \nits digital representation so that it can later be rendered and experienced through immersive devices.\n1. Purpose of Modeling\nIt defines the structure, behavior, and meaning of the elements composing the virtual world. Without \naccurate models, rendering (visual, auditory, haptic) cannot produce coherent or immersive experience.\n2. Types of Rendering\nOnce the world is modeled, the VR system can render it through various sensory channels:\n• Visual Rendering: Transforms 3D models into 2D images displayed to the user.\n• Auditory Rendering: Spatializes sounds based on object position and user orientation.\n• Haptic Rendering: provides tactile or force feedback corresponding to object interaction.\nVR applications combine all three to achieve multisensory immersion.\n3. Modeling Constraints\nA valid virtual world model has to satisfy 6 constraints to ensure realism and coherence. When these \nconstraints are fulfilled, the user perceives the virtual world as credible and become immersed in it:\n1. Geometric consistency – Objects have realistic shape, size, and proportions.\n2. Spatial coherence – Positions, distances, and orientations respect physical logic.\n3. Physical plausibility – Interactions follow basic physical rules (gravity, collision, resistance).\n4. Temporal continuity – Smooth and consistent object movements and transformations over time.\n5. Semantic consistency – Each object’s meaning and function are preserved in context.\n6. Computational efficiency – the model must be optimized for real-time rendering performance.\n4. The Visual Model: The Core\nThe visual model is the foundation of any virtual world. The other modalities (such as sound and haptics) \nare added on top to enrich user perception and enhance immersion. The design of the visual model \ntypically involves four key stages:\n4.1. The Geometric Modeling\nThis model defines the shapes and the structure of objects and spaces using mathematical \nrepresentations (meshes, surfaces, volumes) to describe the world geometry.\n4.2. The Kinematic Modeling\nIt defines the movements and the transformations (translation, rotation, scaling) that affect virtual \nentities over time. This step introduces animation and user-object motion.\n4.3. The Topological Modeling\nThis model defines spatial relationships between the virtual objects — adjacency, connectivity, inclusion.\nIt ensures spatial continuity (e.g., doors connected to rooms, walls enclosing spaces).\n4.4. The Semantic Modeling\nDefines the behavioral and interaction rules: Collisions, interferences, logical relations, user interactions\ngiving meaning to actions. e.g. what happens when the user touches, moves, or manipulates an object.\n\nModeling Type Purpose Impact on Immersion\nGeometric Defines shapes and spaces Supports visual realism\nKinematic Controls motion and animation Increases interactivity\nTopological Structures spatial relationships Enhances spatial presence\nSemantic Defines interaction logic Builds meaning and coherence\nAuditory/Haptic Extend sensory realism Deepen immersion\n5. Integration of Multisensory Models\nOnce the visual core is ready, it’s time to integrate:\n• Sound models (spatialized, reactive audio cues)\n• Haptic models (touch, texture, or force feedback)\nThese additional layers enhance the sense of presence and interactivity, the two pillars of immersion.\n5.1. 🎧✋ Integration of Sound and Haptic Modalities within the Modeling Stages\nThe visual model is the structural foundation, sound and haptic modalities are integrated \nprogressively in the final design — mostly during the semantic stage, but they rely on the information \nbuilt in the previous stages:\n Geometric Modeling\n Base for spatial references\n• Sound sources and haptic zones need positions and shapes to exist in the virtual space.\n• Example: defining where a loudspeaker or a vibrating object is located.\n Sound and haptics are not yet active here, but geometry provides the necessary coordinates.\n Kinematic Modeling\n Movement and transformation of sensory sources\n• When objects move, their associated sounds and haptic feedback have to follow.\n• Example: a drone’s buzzing sound moves with the drone’s trajectory.\n Sound/haptic parameters begin to depend on motion data.\n Topological Modeling\n Spatial and adjacency logic for sensory propagation\n• Defining how sound travels (through doors, between rooms) and where haptic effects apply.\n• Example: footsteps’ sound changes between rooms; vibration passes through connected surfaces.\n Topology defines the logic of sensory continuity and propagation.\n Semantic Modeling\n Integration and activation of multisensory interactions\n• This is where sound and haptic models are truly implemented and controlled, Defining rules, \ntriggers, and responses linking user actions to multisensory outputs\n• Example: When the user touches an object → vibration + impact sound\n Full multisensory integration happens here.\nModeling Stage Role for Sound & Haptics Integration Level\nGeometric Defines spatial location of sensory sources Basic\nKinematic Links sensory cues to object movement Moderate\nTopological Controls sensory propagation and adjacency Moderate\nSemantic Defines interaction rules and sensory feedback Full integration\nSound and haptics are anchored in the geometry, animated in the kinematics, propagated in the topology, and\nactivated in the semantics.\n6. 🧩 Classification of Objects in Virtual Worlds\n1️⃣ Rigid and Non-Deformable Solids 🧱\nObjects that do not deform — Only undergo rigid transformations (translation, rotation, scaling).\n➔ Represent static or mechanical elements such as buildings, furniture, or vehicles.\n Fundamental for geometry, physics, and collisions.\nApproach – Polygonal / Mesh-Based Modeling + Rigid-Body Physic → No vertex deformation.\n2️⃣ Articulated Solids ⚙️\nSystems of rigid parts connected by joints (axes of rotation, sliding, or hierarchy).\n➔ Used for skeletal or mechanical structures: avatars, robots, doors, or arms.\n\n Essential for animation and kinematics.\nApproach – Skeletal Modeling with Forward/Inverse kinematics (FK/IK) + joint constraints.\n3️⃣ Deformable Solids 🧬\nObjects changing shape or volume according to physical or procedural deformation laws.\n➔ Usage: Common for organic materials (skin, muscles, clothes, rubber, vegetation).\n Crucial for realism in soft materials and living beings.\nApproach – Soft-Body Physics, Finite Element Modeling (FEM), Blendshape Animation\n→ Vertex deformation.\n4️⃣ Non-Solids (Continuous Media) 💧\nMatter without a fixed shape or volume: fluids, gases, fire, smoke, or particle systems.\n➔ Used to simulate natural environments, weather, or dynamic visual effects.\n Enhanced through GPU-based simulation and shaders.\nApproach – 🌫️ Particle Systems, Fluid Dynamics (SPH, Navier–Stokes), Volumetric rendering.\n5️⃣ Abstract or Procedural Entities 🌀 (Modern Extension)\nNot physical, logical, interactive, data-driven (e.g., holograms, force fields, UI elements, or AI agents).\n➔ Increasingly used in XR, data visualization, and interactive interfaces.\n Emerging category — bridges modeling and interaction logic.\nModeling Approach – Procedural modeling, event-driven logic, or parametric generation.\nCategory Behavior Examples Modeling Approach\nRigid Solids No deformation Buildings, tools Mesh modeling + rigid-body physics\nArticulated Solids Rigid parts with joints Robots, avatars Skeletal modeling + kinematics\nDeformable Solids Elastic deformation Skin, cloth Soft-body / FEM / blendshapes\nNon-Solids No fixed volume Water, fire, smoke Particle / fluid dynamics\nAbstract Entities Logical / procedural UI, AI, holograms Procedural / event-driven\n7. 🧮 Formats of Geometric Representation in Virtual Worlds\nBack to the geometric model, the core of the virtual world representation: Geometric structures can be \nrepresented using two main approaches: Primitive-based models or Analytical models.\n1️⃣ Primitive-Based Representation 🧱\nBuilt from basic geometric elements combined to form complex shapes. This is the most common format \nused in real-time 3D and VR applications including :\n Vertex-based: Defined by 3D points (vertices) in space.\n Edge-based: Defined by connections between vertices (edges).\n Face-based: Defined by polygonal surfaces (triangles, quads, meshes).\nThese entities (polygonal and meshes) are suitable for interactive rendering and collision detection.\n2️⃣ Analytical Representation 📈\nDescribed using mathematical equations instead of discrete primitives focusing on precision and \ncontinuous surfaces. This includes:\n Parametric models – Surfaces defined by mathematical functions (e.g., Bézier, B-splines, NURBS).\n Used for CAD, organic shapes, and smooth curves.\n Non-parametric models – Defined implicitly or explicitly by equations without parameters.\n🧮 Example: Implicit: F(x,y,z)=0 | Explicit: z=f(x,y)\n Used for volumetric forms, terrains, or implicit surfaces (metaballs).\nEquation-based and procedural models suit scientific visualization, simulation, or precise design.\nRepresentation Basis Typical Use Strengths Limitations\nPrimitive\u0002based\nVertices, edges, \nfaces\nGames, VR, real-time \nscenes\nFast, intuitive, GPU\u0002friendly Less precise, discrete\nAnalytical Mathematical \nequations CAD, simulation Smooth, exact Harder to render in real time"};
window.ATELIER_COURS["1SdINhJNxNaa4n0WNbqW1eDcvmYhZQlhX"] = {"title":"FacialAnimation","body":"University of Science and Technology of Oran – Mohamed Boudiaf (USTOMB) \nFaculty of Mathematics and Informatics\nComputer Science Department\nAnimated Multimedia \nApplications Design\nFacial Animation\nMaster’s Degree Courses\nArtificial Intelligence and ApplicationsWye animating faces ?\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\nEmotional Expressions\nSpeech\n✓ How ?\n✓ Reference –\n➢ The human face\nViewerFacial movements\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\nMandible movements\n❑ Vertical – Lowering/raising: opening/closing mouth\n❑ Anteroposterior – Front & rear chin projection.\n❑ Lateral – Carrying the chin to the left & to the right\nMuscles\n❑ Push/pull skin in different ways\n❑ Direct in creation of expressions\n❑ Chewing, the lips/eyes movements\nThe mouth\n❑ Independent lower/upper lips \n❑ Words, emotions\n❑ Gums, teeth & tongue contribute to expression \nwhen visible in facial posture (secondary in \nanimations but provide more realism).\nEyes & eyebrows\n❑ Convey emotions & create \nexpressions\n❑ Animation constraint – Ensure \nconvergence of directions of gazes \npointed by the two eyes\n❑ Animations – Pupil dilation, Iris \ncoloring, Light reflection\n❑ Accompany with eyebrow movements \nHair, folds, wrinkles, \ncolors\n❑ Character traits\n❑ Hair & hair elements: \nbeard, mustaches ...Emotions\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\nHappiness\nSadness\nAnger\nFear Disgust\nSurprise\nContempt\nBasic \nEmotionsEmotions\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\nSecondary \nEmotions\nConfusion\nWorry\nDisdain Deviousness\nRage\nenthusiasm\nshy\nDisinterest\nTerror WearinessRealistic vs Symbolic\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\nHigh-Fidelity Simulation of Human \nAnatomy\n→ Detailed anatomical structure (bones, \nmuscles, skin layers, soft-tissue \ndynamics)\n→ Biomechanical accuracy – Follow \nnatural constraints : muscle contraction, \nskin elasticity.\n→ High computational precision –\nRequires advanced algorithms, motion \ncapture data, physical simulation\nExample Application –\n→ Medical Training\nCommon Techniques\n→ Parameterized Meshes\n→ Analytical Vector Models\nSimplified, Expressive, Interpretable\n→ Simplified facial components\nReduced to geometric shapes: \nCircles, arcs, lines…\n→ Stylized expressions – Clarity & emotional \nreadability rather than anatomy\n→ Intuitive, exaggerated symbols that audiences \ninterpret instantly\n→ Low computational cost – Suitable for mobile apps, \nstylized games, data vis., UI and pedagogical content.\nEyes – Circles, dots, lines for emotion\nMouth – Arcs indicating emotions\nColors – Symbolically to reinforce mood\n→ Voice tone, Rhythm, pauses, stress, prosody\n→ Compensate for simplicity of the visual \nexpression by adding emotional depth.\nTypical \nElements\nEnhanced \nby AudioVisemes\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\nVisual Counterpart of Speech Sounds\n→ Visual representations of phonemes\n→ Facial movements & mouth shapes\nrequired to simulate speech in \nanimation\nKey Facial Components\nMouth Shape – Primary component – Speech animation\n→ Degree of opening | Rounding or spreading of the lips\n→ Vertical/horizontal deformations\nJaw Position – Particularly for open vowels\n→ Lowering – Open vowels (/a/) | Lifting – Closed/semi-cl. (/i/, /u/)\nTongue Position – Accuracy/realism [Approximated otherwise]\n→ Front, central, or back vowels.\n→ Shapes consonants like /l/, /t/, /d/, /r/.\n→ Enhances credibility of speech animation\nLips and Lip Corners – Lip tension and lip-corner\n→ Rounded vowels (/o/, /u/) | Bilabial consonants (/p/, /b/, /m/)\n→ Fricatives (/f/, /v/) – Lower lip touches upper teeth\nSecondary Facial Movements\n→ Cheek tension during lip stretching\n→ Subtle eyebrow movements\n→ Micro expressions that occur naturally when talking\nImportance of Visemes in Animation\n→ Lip-sync – films, games, VR, avatars\n→ Speech-driven facial animations\n→ Human–computer interaction, \nincluding conversational agents\n→ Multimodal communication research\nintegrating audio & visual cuesFacial Action Coding System (FACS) \nAnatomically based system describing\nobservable facial movements\nExpressions → Action Units (AUs) –\nindividual muscle movements\nPaul Ekman & Wallace V. Friesen\nUsed in psychology, emotion\nresearch, deception detection, & HCI\nEnables precise analysis of emotions\nand nonverbal cues\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\nAU1 – Inner Brow RaiserApplication of FACS\nScientific research – Study emotions, \nnonverbal communication, & social \ninteractions\nAnimators & computer scientists → Facial \nanimation & emotion recognition\nAwareness of subtle facial behaviors\nPsychotherapists, interviewers, educators, & \ncommunication specialists\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)FACS Manual\nCriteria to observe and code each Action Unit (AU)\nExplains how AUs combine for complex expressions\nFirst published in 1978 by Paul Ekman & Wallace V. Friesen\nMost recently revised in 2002\nAvailable from the Paul Ekman Group for professional and \nacademic use\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)Codes for action units\nAUs – Fundamental actions of individual or group of muscles\nIntensity scoring\nA – Trace\nB – Slight\nC – Marked or pronounced\nD – Severe or extreme\nE – Maximum\nFace side\nR – On the right side of the face and L : on the left.\nU – Unilateral unit (only one side) without specific side\nA – An action which is unilateral but has a stronger side\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)Animated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)❑ 1+2+4+5+7+20+26\n❑ 4+5+7+23\n❑ 9+15+17\n❑ R12A+R14A\n❑ Fear\n❑ Anger\n❑ Disgust\n❑ Contempt\n❑ Happiness\n❑ Sadness\n❑ Surprise\nUniversal Emotions\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\n❑ 6+12\n❑ 1+4+15\n❑ 1+2+5B+26AU 0\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\n❑ The face is not showing any action, often \ncalled a neutral face.\n❑ The face is not actually at rest because \nthe eyes are open, the jaw is closed, but \nno AU can be scored.AU 1\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\n❑ The inner corners of the \neyebrows are lifted slightly, \nthe skin of the glabella and \nforehead above it is lifted \nslightly and wrinkles \ndeepen slightly and a trace \nof new ones form in the \ncenter of the forehead.AU 2\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\n❑ The outer part of the \neyebrow raise is pronounced. \nThe wrinkling above the \nright outer eyebrow has \nincreased markedly, and the \nwrinkling on the left is \npronounced. Increased \nexposure of the eye cover \nfold and skin is pronounced.AU 1+2\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\n❑ This action is \nasymmetrical. The entire \neyebrow is raised up \nextremely. The eye cover \nfold is exposed \nextremely.AU 4\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\n❑ Vertical wrinkles appear in \nthe glabella and the \neyebrows are pulled \ntogether.\n❑ The inner parts of the eye\u0002brows are pulled down a \ntrace on the right and slightly \non the left with traces of \nwrinkling at the corners.AU 1+4\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\n❑ Raising the inner\ncorners of the\neyebrows and pull the\neyebrows together.AU 1+2+4\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\n❑ The entire brow is raised\nmarkedly. The slight\npulling together\nappears weaker than\nthe raising, with only\nslight straightening and\na trace of bulging\nbetween the brows.AU 5\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\n❑ The upper eyelid is pulled \nup, the medial part \nvirtually disappearing into \nthe revulsion margin of the \neye cover fold. More sclera \naround the upper part of \nthe iris is revealed\n❑ The inner corner of the eye \nshows more mucosa and \nscleraAU 1+2+5\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\n❑ Eye brows raised to \nexpose the eye cover fold. \nThe upper eyelid is raised \nto expose virtually the \nentire iris.AU 4+5\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\n❑ Pull the inner parts ofthe \neyebrows are pulled \ntogether and raise the \nupper eyelid.AU \n1+2+4+5\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)"};
window.ATELIER_COURS["1Q3DqD_beUcV9PBTp8Z_J7hN3B5R_bOez"] = {"title":"AMM Applications","body":"Available on zenodo – DOI : 10.5281/zenodo.17387686\nMemorandum on Animated Multimedia Application Design\nIntroduction to Animated Applications\nAuthor: Abdelkrim Mebarki\nMaster’s Program in Computer Science – Artificial Intelligence & Applications\nDepartment of Computer Science – Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science\nUniversity of Science and Technology of Oran\nOctober 18, 2025\n1. Application domains\nAnimated Multimedia Applications are present in every domain of informatics. They play crucial \nroles whenever communication engages multiple modalities and multiple dimensions of human \nperception. Here are some application domains:\n1.1. Artificial Intelligence and Intelligent Assistance\nApplied in games, software guidance, and e-governance platforms to improve decision-making, \nautomate management processes, and personalize user experiences.\n1.2. E-Learning\nDevelopment of interactive educational material, didactic courses, and multimedia demonstrations \nthat facilitate remote and lifelong learning making it more efficient.\n1.3. Accessibility\nDesign of software, games, and applications adapted for people with disabilities (e.g., visually \nimpaired or hearing-impaired users), improving inclusivity and equal access to digital resources.\n1.4. Extended Reality (VR – AR)\nCreation of immersive and mixed environments that simulate real or imagined scenarios for training, \nentertainment, therapy, and exploration.\n1.5. Performance and Decision Support\nSolutions that optimize usage efficiency, assist in complex and tough decision-making, and provide \nanalytical support in professional contexts.\n1.6. Communication\nEnrichment of traditional communication channels through multimedia elements such as video \nconferencing, interactive media, and social platforms.\n1.7. Scientific Applications\nUse of simulations (e.g., chemical reactions, physical processes) and advanced data and \ninformation visualization to support research, experimentation, and knowledge dissemination.\n2. What is Computer Animation?\nComputer animations are digital generation of motion and transformation within a multimedia scene, \nenabling the visualization of changes in objects or hollow entities over time. They provide a dynamic \nrepresentation that goes beyond static imagery by simulating temporal evolution.\n2.1. Underlying Nature\nThe physical world is naturally dynamic, ruled by time-dependent phenomena such as movement, \ntransformation, and interaction. Capturing this dynamic concept is essential for accurately reflecting \nreality or building convincing virtual scenarios.\n2.2. Perceptual Dimension\nHuman beings perceive dynamic scenes directly through their sensory systems, particularly vision \nand hearing, which are highly sensitive to movement and temporal variation. Computer animations \nexploit this perceptual capacity and present visual sequences that align with natural cognitive \nprocesses, thereby creating a continuous sense of intuitive motion.\n\n2.3. Dynamic in multimedia modalities\nThe vision is a perceptual modality that is intrinsically spatial rather than inherently dynamic. The \nhuman perceives the world through static snapshots captured at discrete units in time; motion and \ndynamic change are interpreted by the brain as a succession of these static frames processed \nover time. On the other hand, sound and haptic perception are fundamentally dynamic, as they \ndepend on temporal variation for their definition and perception. It is clear that there is no existence \nof “static sound” or “static touch sensation” in the physical or perceptual sense. Both of the two \nmodalities require continuous change over time (pressure fluctuations for sound, or force and \nmotion variations for haptics) to be perceived. While vision constructs motion from stillness, audition \nand touch can only exist through movement and temporal evolution.\n3. How to create a computer animation?\nTo create an animation, we must follow a pipeline defined over three stages, each including one \nor more steps:\n3.1. Static Definition\nThis stage involves the design of the virtual environment and its components via two steps:\n➔ Asset creation: Modeling, texturing, scanning, photography, or importing ready-made \nassets.\n➔ Scene setup: Placing objects in the virtual environment, defining lighting and spatial \nrelationships.\n→ The resulting models correspond to the static virtual world.\n3.2. Dynamic Virtual World\nIn this stage, we introduce motion and transformation via one step:\n➔ Animation: Introducing movement through keyframes, motion capture, procedural \nanimation, or physics-based simulations.\n→ Here the scenario governs how objects/entities evolve over time. This matches your \nscenario-driven dynamic world.\n3.3. Computer Animation (Perceptual Rendering)\nIn this stage, we translate the dynamic scene into perceivable motion via two steps:\n➔ Rendering: Computing and displaying frames that visualize the dynamic world.\n➔ Post-production: Adding effects, synchronization, and refinement to create continuity and \nrealism.\n→ This produces the illusion of movement perceived by the user.\n4. Animation classifications\nAnimation can be classified according to two main dimensions: the mode of execution and the \nmode of use.\n4.1. By Mode of Execution (How the animation is produced)\n➔ Frame-by-frame animation → Traditional cinema, cartoons, stop-motion (each frame \ndrawn/recorded individually).\n➔ Automated/Procedural animation → Generated automatically by rules, physics, \nalgorithms, or AI (e.g., simulating fluids, growing a tree, ragdoll physics).\n4.2. By Mode of Use (How the animation is delivered/consumed)\n➔ Real-time animation → Computed on the fly, responding to user input (e.g., video games, \nVR, simulators).\n➔ Pre-rendered animation → Precomputed and played back as a sequence of frames (e.g., \nanimated films, cinematic cutscenes).\n5. Interactivity in Multimedia Applications\n\nInteractivity is a key concept in any multimedia application if user immersion or engagement is \nsought.\n5.1. Definition and Scope\nInteractivity in multimedia applications is the capacity of the system to adapt the rendering of its \ncontent in real time according to the user actions, inputs, and behaviors. Unlike non-interactive\nmedia, which present fixed and predetermined content, interactive multimedia generates or modifies \ncontent dynamically, creating a feedback loop continuous over time between system and user.\n5.2. Synchronized Rendering\nA distinctive feature of advanced interactive application is the synchronization between user inputs \nand actions and media rendering. In such contexts, the content is not static but generated or \nmodified on the fly in response to the user’s behavior. Examples include:\n➔ Virtual Reality (VR) environments and games – User head and body movements instantly \nmodify the viewpoint and the scene rendering.\n➔ AI-powered support assistants – Dynamically adjust dialogues, visualizations, or \nrecommendations depending on user queries.\n➔ Training simulators (e.g., flight or surgical simulators) – Every user action has an immediate \neffect on the simulated environment.\nThis synchronized interactivity transforms media from a passive and static communication channel \ninto a responsive and adaptive system, enhancing immersion, personalization, and learning \neffectiveness.\n5.3. Technical Realization of Interactive Rendering\nThe implementation of synchronized interactivity relies on four components:\nInput Capture\n➔ Devices such as controllers, motion sensors, eye trackers, or microphones capture user \nactions in real time.\n➔ These signals are continuously sampled and transmitted to the application.\nProcessing and Interpretation\n➔ The system interprets the user input through computing algorithms, including physics \nengines, gesture recognition, natural language processing, and AI decision-making models.\n➔ Predictive models can be used to anticipate user actions, allowing proactive adaptation of \ncontent.\nContent Generation\n➔ The multimedia content elements (graphics, animations, sounds, or haptic feedback) are \ndynamically generated or updated based on the interpreted input.\n➔ Rendering engines (2D/3D graphics pipelines, audio engines) ensure that the response of \nthe system is perceptually seamless.\nFeedback Delivery\n➔ The adapted content is rendered back to the user through visual, auditory, or tactile \nperipherals.\n➔ Synchronization ensures that the feedback latency remains below the human perceptual \nthresholds, maintaining immersion and engagement.\n✅ In summary: Interactive multimedia applications go beyond the static content delivery by establishing a \nreal-time, synchronized deal loop between the user and the system. This deal is achieved through the \ncontinuous capture, interpretation, generation, and feedback of multimedia data and decisions. The result is \na dynamic and adaptive environment where user actions and inputs directly shape the evolution of the \ncontent, enabling user engagement and immersive experiences such as VR and AR, AI-driven assistance, \nand advanced training simulations."};
window.ATELIER_COURS["1NdIrrz6IDqwr9biz9Przp-hrCG-dxTos"] = {"title":"BeyondVision","body":"Available on zenodo – DOI : 10.5281/zenodo.17379102\nMemorandum on Animated Multimedia Application Design\nMultisensory Modalities beyond Vision\nAuthor: Abdelkrim Mebarki\nMaster’s Program in Computer Science – Artificial Intelligence & Applications\nDepartment of Computer Science – Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science\nUniversity of Science and Technology of Oran\nOctober 17, 2025\n1. Sound modality\nSound is a mechanical vibration that propagates through a medium (air, water, solid or fluid \nmaterials) and is ultimately perceived by the human ear directly as an auditory sensation. Sound \nvibrations generate pressure waves travelling outward from the source in all geometric directions. \nWhen the waves reach the human ear, they stimulate the eardrum and the auditory system, \nconverting the mechanical energy into neural signals that the brain interprets as sound. \n1.1. Modeling\nA sound source produces vibrations travelling through a medium and are captured by a\nmicrophone → This device converts the mechanical vibrations into electrical signals → The signal \nis analyzed and transformed into digital form through two main computer operations: Sampling and \nQuantization → Sampling involves taking discrete measurements of the continuous sound wave \nat several regular intervals → Quantization assigns numerical values (codes) to the sampled points, \nenabling its storage and manipulation by the different digital systems.\n1.2. Digital parameters\nTo reproduce sound accurately, the sampling rate must be at least twice the highest perceived \nfrequency present in the sampled signal according to the Nyquist theorem. Since the human \nhearing range is [20 Hz – 2 KHz], the standard audio systems adopt a sampling rate of 44.1 kHz.\nThe bit depth, determines the number of bits we use to encode each sampled value. This number\ndirectly influence the precision and dynamic range of the recorded sound. Higher bit depth allows \nus for finer distinctions in amplitude, resulting in a smoother, more precise recordings with less \nquantization noise in the produced sound.\n1.3. Sound utility\nSound contributes both in semantic meaning and contextual richness of the user experience:\n➔ Carrier of explicit information: Speech, dialogue, narration, music (directly communicate \nmessages, emotions, or narrative content). The mentioned auditory elements guide the user’s \nunderstanding and enhance the expressiveness of multimedia presentations.\n➔ Contextual and environmental function: Through spatial cues (directionality, distance, \nreverberation, and ambient noise). Sound helps audience perceiving virtual spaces, realism, \nand VR immersion and presence. Subtle auditory details, such as footsteps approaching \nfrom behind or fading echo in a large hall, also provide the brain with necessary information \nabout the context and environment contributing to the overall sense of presence.\n1.4. Advanced tools\n➔ Recording and playback systems capture sound waves using microphones or audio sensors \nand reproduce them through speakers ensuring that the original acoustic properties are \npreserved as faithfully as possible. Beyond simple sound recording.\n➔ Synthesis and transformation tools (Digital synthesizers, samplers, and audio processors) \nallow the generation of entirely new original sounds or the alteration of existing ones, providing \ncomposers and designers with large creative possibilities and choices.\n\n➔ Sound effects (SFX) tools are used for enhancing realism or evoke specific emotions in \nmultimedia productions, like the sound of footsteps in a movie or the different simulated \nambiances of virtual environments. Following this, e\n➔ Editing and montage enable accurate temporal and structural ordering of the audio tracks to \nensure the wright synchronization with visual elements or narrative cues.\n➔ Mixing and mastering processes adjust sound levels, equalize frequencies, and apply \ncompression or spatial effects to attain clarity, depth, and a professional finish suitable for end\u0002user public distribution.\n1.5. Implementation\nSound poses more significant storage and processing demands than text modality, owing to its \ncontinuous nature and high data rate.\nFor example, a single minute of uncompressed high-quality audio require several megabytes of \nstorage, which historically limited its use in early digital systems.\nSound remains one of the most pervasive and powerful channels in all multimedia applications, \ndistinguished by its expressive, immersive, and emotive characteristics. Unlike other modalities, \nit instantly conveys mood, urgency, or atmosphere, enhancing comprehension and engagement.\nThe development of advanced compression techniques (MP3 – MPEG-1 Audio Layer III and\nAAC – Advanced Audio Coding) has revolutionized sound storage and transmission reducing file \nsizes dramatically and eliminating inaudible frequencies and redundant data while maintaining \nperceptual audio quality through robust psychoacoustic representations. In combination with\nefficient encoding and streaming methods, these advanced tools have made the diffusion of \naudio content (Digital music, podcasts, online education…) feasible on a large global scale.\n2. Haptic modality\nThis modality is intricate and multifaceted. Unlike sound/image primarily processed through distinct \nsensory organs (ear/eye), Haptic engages the entire body as a perceptual interface. It integrates \ncutaneous sensations (touch, pressure, temperature, and texture) and proprioceptive feedback\n(Awareness of body position, posture, orientation, and movement). This multifaced nature makes \nhaptic perception rich and complex Combining external contact with internal bodily awareness.\nCognitive perspective: Haptic perception emerges through the integration of somatic stimuli\n(Skin receptors, muscles, and joints) with audiovisual information processed by the brain. The \nresulting multimodal synthesis creates a unified haptic sensory experience allowing humans to \ninteract with their environment in a realistic and embodied way. The haptic sense is thus essential\nfor spatial understanding, object manipulation, and also emotional engagement, making a \nbridge between physical interaction and cognitive interpretation.\nExample: In a VR medical training environment, haptic feedback devices (force-feedback gloves\nor surgical simulators) allow users to really feel tissue resistance, instrument vibrations, and \npressure changes in medical acts. These tactile cues, combined with visual and auditory elements, \ncreate realistic learning experience enhancing manual precision, spatial awareness, and\nsensorimotor coordination that replicates the conditions of real surgical practice.\n3. Olfaction and Gustation\n✅ What we can call chemical senses (olfaction or smell and gustation or taste) play an important role \nin human perception and contribute to survival and the richness of sensory human experience.\n→ These two modalities are closely interrelated\n→ They constitute the foundation of flavor perception, influencing memory, emotion, and behavior.\nOlfaction is the biological process by which the human detects and identifies odors – Gustation\ncomplements the olfaction by recognizing chemical substances dissolved in food or drink.\n\n3.1. Biological mechanism\nFirst, the biological mechanism of Olfaction (the Sense of Smell):\n→ Starts in olfactory epithelium – Thin, yellowish membrane high within the human nasal cavity.\n→ This epithelium contains millions of olfactory receptor neurons specialized to bind with specific\nairborne chemical molecules.\n→ When these molecules are inhaled, they interact with the receptors, initiating a cascade of\nelectrochemical signals that travel to the olfactory bulb (a neural structure responsible for the\ninitial processing and organization of olfactory information).\n→ The signals are then conveyed to higher brain regions, including the olfactory cortex and the\nlimbic system, where they are allied with memory and emotion.\n→ This is why certain smells can evoke vivid recollections or emotional states.\nSecond, the biological mechanism of Gustation (Sense of Taste):\n→ The taste buds, located on the tongue, soft palate, and oral mucosa, contain clusters of \nspecialized gustatory receptor cells that respond to the different dissolved molecules.\n→ They detect the five primary taste known qualities: sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and umami.\n→ Each quality provides critical information about the nutritional or toxic value of what is ingested.\n→ The sensory information is then transmitted through the gustatory nerves to the gustatory \ncortex, where the brain interprets these signals and produces the conscious experience of taste.\n3.2. Utility\nOlfaction is rarely implemented in current multimedia systems although possesses an exceptional \nability to stimulate memory and emotion. The olfactory system’s direct connection to the \nlimbic structures of the brain allows scents to activate vivid recollections and emotional responses \nmore effectively than traditional visual or auditory cues. In VR/AR contexts, olfactory feedback might\nsignificantly strengthen the presence sense, making simulated environments feel more authentic \nand more psychologically engaging.\nFor instance, the scent of sea salt in a coastal VR simulation or the aroma of wood in a medieval \nsetting anchor users more deeply into the virtual experience, fostering immersion with contextual \nrealism and emotional resonance.\nGustation remains less explored due to technical and physiological complexity and \nconstraints. Its potential applications are recognized in specialized medical and cognitive fields.\nCulinary simulations and gastronomy training platforms could use digital taste interfaces to \nteach flavor composition and palate development. In medical rehabilitation, gustatory feedback \nmight assist patients recovering from taste loss or eating disorders via reactivating sensory \npathways. Furthermore, in experimental entertainment and multisensory storytelling, taste \ncues could be synchronized with narrative or visual rendering to expand the expressive range of \nnumerical media to create novel aesthetic and emotional experiences.\nExample – Immersive dining experiences: VR headsets are combined with scent diffusers and \nflavor-modulating devices. Participants might visually perceive a tropical forest, smell exotic fruit \naromas, and taste a corresponding dish (all aligned to the storyline). Such multisensory coherence \nillustrates how olfaction and gustation can transform traditional media into embodied immersive \nexperiences, bridging the gap between perception, emotion, and technology.\n3.3. Human capacity\nHumans can perceive five universally recognized basic tastes:\n1. Sweet: For sugars and energy-rich foods;\n2. Sour: Related to acids and the balance of pH;\n3. Salty: For mineral salts and electrolyte regulation;\n\n4. Bitter: Serving as a natural warning signal for potentially toxic substances;\n5. Umami: Savory derived from amino acids (glutamate) commonly found in protein-rich foods.\nIn the olfaction realm, humans are remarkably sensitive to around 10,000 distinct odors, and \nresearch suggests that olfactory system may distinguish up to one trillion olfactory stimuli. This \nextraordinary capacity underlines human olfactory perception richness and complexity and \nemphasizes how taste and smell together shape the experiences of flavor, emotion, and memory.\n3.4. Challenges and limitations\n➔ Perceptual and Physical Constraints\nSmell and taste lack a standardized perceptual scale or measurable perceptual range, which makes \nit difficult to reproduce or precisely control and imitate the corresponding sensations. The chemical \nsynthesis, diffusion, and storage of odors require compounds that are both stable and non\u0002toxic, adding more complexity. Moreover, environmental factors such as ambient humidity, \ntemperature, and airflow can significantly alter the perceived intensity and quality of a scent.\n➔ Ergonomic Constraints\nIntegrating olfactory and gustatory feedback into multimedia applications raises important and \nconflictual ergonomic issues. Artificial scents may interfere with or contradict other modality stimuli \n(visual and auditory), potentially disrupting the sense of immersion. Furthermore, taste and smell \ndelivery systems must be accurately controlled and highly localized (particularly within the tongue \nor nasal cavity) to prevent user discomfort, cross-contamination, and overstimulation.\n➔ Cognitive Constraints\nThe persistence and slow diffusion of odors and taste pose a major challenge for temporal \nsynchronization with the other sensory modalities. Once released, smells and taste linger in the \nenvironment or the mouth and are difficult to remove quickly, which complicates real-time \nadaptation and interactivity. Misalignment between olfactory, visual, and auditory cues can lead \nto perceptual contradictions, reducing realism and confusing the user’s sensory immersive \nexperience.\n3.5. Advanced Tools and Research\n➔ Advances in Olfactory Simulation\nRecent research has led to the emergence of what we can call olfactory displays, specialized \ndevices capable of emitting controlled and programmable scents. Complementing these are\nmicro-diffusers allowing targeted and localized scent delivery, enhancing realism and user \nimmersion. Furthermore, wearable olfactory systems are increasingly being integrated into VR\nand AR applications, enabling the synchronization of smell with visual and auditory cues.\nExperimental research is also expanding in cross-modal perception, investigating how olfactory \nstimuli interact with other sensory modalities (vision, sound, and haptic) to create richer and more \ncoherent multisensory experiences.\n➔ Advances in Gustatory Simulation\nThe gustatory dimension has seen the rise of electronic tongues: Peripherals capable of\nanalyzing, recognizing, and replicating taste profiles through advanced sensing solutions.\nResearchers are also exploring electrostimulation techniques applied directly to the taste buds\nto evoke or modulate taste sensations without chemical substances or direct end-contact. This \nadvancement is paving the way for wearable gustatory interfaces designed for VR and AR \nenvironments and offering new opportunities for culinary education, sensory rehabilitation, and \nimmersive entertainment through the digital recreation of taste experiences."};
window.ATELIER_COURS["1n_zftXTNMijTt_m72GWpmMQ7tktH25n0"] = {"title":"AnimationTechniques","body":"University of Science and Technology of Oran – Mohamed Boudiaf (USTOMB) \nFaculty of Mathematics and Informatics\nComputer Science Department\nAnimated Multimedia \nApplications Design\nAnimation technique principles\nMaster’s Degree Courses\nArtificial Intelligence and ApplicationsBuilding animations\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\nTiming Posing Spacing\n✓ How long it takes to \nhappen ?\n✓ Key poses (Pressing the \npause button)\n✓ The still image !\n✓ Relationship between still images over time\n✓ The space between the framesAnimation keys\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\n✓ Frames\n❑ 24 or 30 frames per seconds\n✓ Poses\n❑ Shots in Frame by Frame animations\n❑ Keyframes in automated generated animations\n❑ Keys, extremes, breakdowns, inbetweens\nPose Pose Pose\nFrame Frame Frame Frame Frame Frame Frame Frame Frame Frame Frame\nFrame Frame Frame Frame Frame Frame Frame Frame Frame Frame FrameAnimation keys\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\n✓ Speed\n❑ Seems fast when the poses are far \napart\n❑ Seems slow when the poses are close \ntogether\n✓ Acceleration & Deceleration\n❑ Speed up: Closer and closer together\n❑ Slow down: Further and further apart \nfrom each other\n✓ Squash & Stretch\n❑ More realistic animations\n❑ Keep the object volume consistent\n✓ Anticipation\n❑ Prepare the action\n❑ Highlight what will be animated\n✓ Overshoot\n❑ Exaggerating or springiness\n❑ Follow through, overlapping, and drag \nactions\n✓ Continuity and curves\n❑ Add flow and fluidity to the animationAnimation keys – Staging\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\nWe must guide the watcher towards \nthe actionAnimation keys – Staging\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\nIncludes Acting, Timing, View \nposition, SettingAnimation keys – Staging\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\nIntroduce the actors one by one according to scenario order: \nThe viewer must not get confused about where to look !Animation keys – Staging\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\nFar away for actions, close up for \nfacial expression Animation keys – Staging\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\nMain action in the middle or in \nthe third of the screenAnimation keys – Staging\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\nMain action clear an simple without \nbeing upstaged by other actionsAnimation keys – Staging\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\nRespect timing, one action at time ! \nNo overlap ! Even put pauses !Animation keys – Staging\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\nKeep displayed text on the \nscreen to be read three times ! Animation keys – Solid drawing\nAnimated Multimedia Applications Design (A. Mebarki)\n✓ Solid Drawing\n❑ Give volume to 2D drawings\n✓ Appeal (Using dynamic design)\n❑ Variety of shapes,\n❑ Proportions (Magnifying interesting parts),\n❑ Keep simple (Symbolism for animating, details for illustrations)"};
window.ATELIER_COURS["1oUmwBQt4w31Q57pdlc80S537hstgyOKn"] = {"title":"Animation Pipeline","body":"Available on zenodo – DOI : 10.5281/zenodo.18060487\nMemorandum on Animated Multimedia Application Design\nAnimation Pipeline\nAuthor: Abdelkrim Mebarki\nMaster’s Program in Computer Science – Artificial Intelligence & Applications\nDepartment of Computer Science – Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science\nUniversity of Science and Technology of Oran\nDecember 26, 2025\nIn this context, it is essential to understand how animations are created. Each animation \ntechnique, whether traditional or digital, defines its creative process, tools, and workflow. Knowing \nthese techniques helps students grasp the diversity of the artistic approaches and the technical \nrequirements that shape every particular project. By exploring the different ways perceived motion \ncan be produced — from the hand-drawn frames to the real-time 3D simulations — learners \ndevelop a complete sense of what’s possible, what resources are needed, and which given \nmethods best suit a given multimedia application.\nUnderstanding the “techniques” provides the foundation for understanding the “process”.\n1. Global Classification of Animation Techniques\n1.1. Traditional & Manual Animation Techniques\nThese techniques rely on the handcrafted artistic processes and include:\n❖ Cut-Out Animation\nFlat characters or objects cut from paper or photos are moved frame by frame under a camera.\n❖ Chinese Shadow (Silhouette Animation)\nUses backlighting to project moving silhouettes. This is inspired by traditional shadow theatre.\n❖ Painting, Pastel, and Sand Animation\nCreated/modified on glass, paper or sand, frame by frame, producing fluid, textural motion.\n❖ Drawing on Film (Direct Animation)\nArtists draw, scratch, or paint directly onto film strips, creating abstract or experimental effects.\n❖ Cartoon Animation (Cel Animation)\nClassic hand-drawn — Scenes are drawn on transparent sheets photographed frame by frame.\n❖ Rotoscoping\nAnimators trace over live-action footage to reproduce realistic movement.\nRotoscoping is an animation technique where animators trace over live-action footage \nframe by frame to reproducing a realistic movement. This is often used to capture natural \nhuman motion, refine the complex gestures, or integrate the animated elements into real filmed \nscenes.\nFor example –\n➔ In traditional animation, artists projected the filmed images onto a light table and drew over \nthem (Disney used this in his work Snow White).\n➔ In modern digital workflows, software like After Effects or Blender allows digital rotoscoping \nto isolate subjects or recreate the motion paths.\n❖ Collage Animation\nAnimated compositions created from the magazine cut-outs, any textures or photos.\n❖ Paint-on-Glass Animation\nWet paints are moved and photographed frame by frame on glass surface for smooth transitions.\n1.2. Stop-Motion and Physical Animation\nAnimation created by photographing real, physical objects frame by frame.\n❖ Puppets and Figurines\nThree-dimensional puppets are moved incrementally and photographed to create motion.\n\n❖ Clay Animation (Claymation)\nThis technique uses malleable clay models for flexible and expressive motion.\nClay Animation (Claymation) is a stop-motion animation technique using malleable \nclay figures or models to create motion. Each character or object is sculpted from plasticine \nor modeling clay; it is placed on a miniature set and photographed frame by frame. Between \neach two shots, the animator slightly moves the clay model — and when all frames are played \nin sequence, the figures in the scene appear to move naturally. This is widely known for its \nexpressive, handmade aesthetic and its ability to give life to tactile, textured characters.\nFamous examples –\n➔ Wallace and Gromit (Aardman Animations)\n➔ Chicken Run\n➔ The Nightmare Before Christmas (mixing clay and puppets)\n❖ Pixilation\nIn this technique, we use live human actors as frame-by-frame “puppets,” for surreal \nmovements.\nPixilation (pixillation) is a stop-motion animation technique where real humans are used \nas if they were stop-motion puppets. The actors pose, move slightly between each two shotd, \nand are photographed frame by frame for a jerky, surreal, or magical effect when played.\nExample – Neighbours (1952) by Norman McLaren (a classic example).\n❖ Object Animation\nEveryday objects are animated frame by frame to create illusion of life.\n❖ Time-Lapse Animation\nCaptures slow processes (like a flower blooming) over long periods, then speeds them up.\nThis is a filming technique (It is not a stop-motion, but similar in spirit) where a camera \ncaptures images at long time intervals — For example, one frame every few seconds, \nminutes, or even hours — and then plays them back at normal speed.\nThe result is a slow process like a flower blooming, a cloud moving, or a building being \nconstructed, appear to happen very fast, almost magically.\n1.3. Digital & Computer-Based Animation\nThis technique family relies on digital tools and software to create or manipulate movement.\n❖ 2D Digital Animation\nVector or bitmap graphics animated digitally (Using Adobe Animate, Toon Boom or Synfig).\n❖ 3D Animation\nObjects modeled in 3D and animated virtually using keyframes rigging, and rendering.\n❖ Motion Graphics\nAnimated shapes, icons or text —Synchronized with sound for multimedia interfaces or\nadvertising.\n❖ Kinetic Typography\nAnimated text conveying emotion, rhythm, or emphasis in multimedia presentations.\n❖ Morphing\nSmooth homeomorphic transformation from one image or shape into another.\n❖ Procedural Animation\nMotion generated algorithmically or through physics simulations.\n❖ Rotoscoped Digital Animation\nModern digital tracing of live-action motion for realistic-like motion (For stylized games and films).\n1.4. Interactive & Multimedia-Specific Animation\nThese animations are designed for interactive real-time environments.\n❖ Scripted (Code-Based) Animation\nGenerated dynamically by programming (JavaScript, Unity, Unreal, Blender scripts).\n\n❖ Data-Driven Animation\nMovement or visuals change according to data inputs (sound, sensors, user input).\n❖ UI/UX Micro-Animations\nSmall interface animations guiding users or giving feedback (buttons, transitions, hover effects).\n❖ AR/VR Animations\nAnimated 3D elements integrated into immersive or mixed reality environments.\n❖ Real-Time Engine Animation\nUses game engines for live rendering and interactivity (Unreal, Unity, Godot).\n1.5. Hybrid & Experimental Techniques\nHere, we combine multiple methods or explore unconventional materials.\n❖ Mixed-Media Animation\nBlends live-action, drawing, photography, and CGI in a single multimedia piece.\n❖ Experimental & Abstract Animation\nFocuses on rhythm, motion, and color rather than narrative (optical or musical visualizations).\n❖ Augmented Performance Animation\nAnimation linked to physical gestures, sensors, or live motion capture.\n2. The Animation Production Pipeline\nThe general production pipeline for creating 2D or 3D animations follows always three major \nphases: Pre-production, Production, and Post-production. Each stage is essential for \nensuring both artistic quality and technical consistency of the final animated scene sequence.\n2.1. Pre-Production Stage\nThis phase defines the creative and the conceptual framework of the final animation involving\nplanning, visual development, and narrative construction before any motion is really produced:\n❖ Scenario Specification (Story Development)\nThe story or multimedia content provides the foundation of the animation. This stage defines:\n➔ Synopsis (Overview) – That describes the nature of the plot, the setting (time & space), \nthe main events, and all the characters involved.\n➔ Sequencer – It breaks down the story scene by scene to guide visual and temporal \norganization.\n❖ Character Design (Workbook Development)\nIt gives visual identity and personality to the protagonists and secondary figures. This stage \ndefines:\n➔ Model Sheet (Character Board) – Depicts each character’s physical traits, attitudes, and \ntypical poses through progressively: Skeletal structure → Simplified form → Finalized \nversion.\n➔ Turnarounds – A set of standard views (Front, Profile, Back, Three-Quarter Face, and \nThree-Quarter Back) used to maintain consistency in drawing and modeling over the\nwhole process.\n➔ Facial Expression Board – Illustrates the full range of emotions and expressions.\n➔ Lip-Sync and Mouth Chart – Maps visemes (visual equivalents of phonemes) to ensure \naccurate synchronization between speech and animation.\n❖ Storyboard Specification\nThe storyboard visually translates the content scenario into a sequence of illustrated frames \n(thumbnails) that serves as a blueprint for the following production process. Each panel indicates:\n➔ The action & its temporal rhythm.\n➔ Camera angles & framing.\n➔ Locations, atmosphere, & lighting intentions.\n➔ Character movements, dialogues, & sound effects.\n➔ Emotional tone conveyed by the scene.\n\n2.2. Production Stage\nThis main phase transforms the conceptual material into animated sequences through the visual \ncreation and the motion synthesis.\n❖ Setting Up Decorative Elements\nHere, we design and organize sets, backgrounds, and environments according to each shot \nor layer. Fixed entities and decorative components are established to provide spatial coherence.\n❖ Animation Process (25–30 frames per second)\nThe animation involves generating motion through the successive produced frames:\n➔ Key Poses Generation – The main animators create essential poses defining the movement.\n➔ Refinement of Key Poses – The assistant animators clean and finalize the key drawings.\n➔ Interpolation (Inbetweening) – Inbetweeners produce the intermediate frames (tweens) that \nensure smooth transitions between the key poses.\n❖ Coloring\nConsist of applying color to characters and backgrounds according to the visual identity defined \nduring pre-production. This may involve digital painting, texture mapping, or shading processes.\n2.3. III. Post-Production Stage\nThis final phase integrates audiovisual components and ensures the coherence of the animation.\n➔ Shooting (Compositing) – Assembly of the visual layers (characters, backgrounds, effects) \ninto a final animated sequence.\n➔ Sound Integration – Recording, synchronization, and mixing of dialogues, sound effects, and \nmusic to support the content visual rhythm.\n➔ Editing – Adjustment in timing, transitions, and visual effects achieving narrative/aesthetic \nunity.\nStage Main Focus Key Outputs\nPre-Production Concept and planning Script, character sheets, storyboard\nProduction Animation creation Animated sequences, colored assets\nPost-Production Integration and finalization Final video, sound mix, editing\n3. Organization, Cost, and Creative Constraints\nThe production of an animation (traditional, digital, or 3D) is a high complex and resource\u0002intensive process. Beyond its underlying artistic dimension, it mobilizes significant human, \ntemporal, and financial resources, requiring a correct coordination of multiple specialized roles \nand technologies. The animation pipeline, structured into pre-production, production, and post\u0002production, functions as a creative system and as an industrial workflow at the same time.\n3.1. A Costly and Time-Consuming Process\nThe animation production is inherently expensive in time and labor. Each second of animation \nmay require dozens of drawings or rendered frames, detailed scene compositions, and \nsynchronized sound. The duration and quality of the final product depend directly on:\n➔ The number of involved professionals (artists, animators, sound engineers, editors),\n➔ The complexity of motion and effects,\n➔ The level of realism or stylistic refinement pursued.\nIn traditional and digital 2D animation, the process remains largely manual, despite a partial use \nof automation tools. In 3D production, while rendering and rigging pipelines reduce some \nrepetition, setup, modeling, and rendering times still represent a major cost factor.\n3.2. The Human Structure: Key Animators, Assistants, and Inbetweeners\nWithin production phase, animation team is hierarchically organized around creative functions:\n➔ Key (Lead) Animators – They define the principal poses, gestures, and emotional states —\nKey animators give rhythm and personality to the movement.\n➔ Assistant Animators – They refine these poses, ensure continuity, and adjust the timing and \nthe proportion between the shots.\n\n➔ Inbetweeners – They create the intermediate frames (or “tweens”) that ensure smooth motion \nand fluid transitions.\nThis hierarchical organization is essential to guarantee precision, consistency, and efficiency\nin large productions where multiple sequences are handled simultaneously by different teams. In \ndigital workflows, the same structure often persists except of “inbetweening”, that can be partially \nautomated through interpolation algorithms.\n3.3. Between Creation & Automation\nThe animation production is a project that lies at the intersection of human creativity and \ntechnical automation. At its core, it remains an art of movement and expression, requiring \nhuman imagination, aesthetic sensitivity, and mastery of drawing and modeling abilities. The \ncreation and animation stages depend on the artist’s ability to translate emotions, rhythm, and \nstory into visual motion.\nYet, as the process evolves from storyboard to animation, the automation progressively \nintervenes. The storyboard acts as a blueprint that guides camera angles, timing, and the shot \ncomposition. The software tools automate repetitive aspects: Interpolation, coloring, or layer \ncompositing. But theses tools never replace the creative decision-making that drives the \nexpressive and the narrative coherence.\n3.4. The Logic of Shot-by-Shot Production and Layer Reusability\nAnimation progresses shot by shot (or scene by scene). This modular organization enables a \nbetter management of complexity, where each shot can be designed, animated, and rendered \nindependently.\nA key optimization technique is the reusability of layers — backgrounds, character rigs, and \neffects can be reused across multiple scenes in order to save time and ensure visual consistency.\nIn 2D animation, for instance, the fixed backgrounds are often separated from the moving \ncharacters to allow only specific layers to change from one frame to another. In 3D animation, \nthis principle translates into asset libraries and modular scene components.\nHowever, this efficiency has some limits: reusability must not compromise the organic flow and \nthe emotional authenticity. Excessive reuse can lead to mechanical and repetitive movement.\n3.5. The Constraint of Non-Modifiable Visual Support\nA particular difficulty of traditional and raster-based digital animation lies in its non-revisable \ndefinition once frames are finalized. Unlike vector graphics or 3D models, which can be modified \ndynamically, traditional drawings and painted frames constitute non-modifiable visual \nsupports: They have not editable source once rendered.\nThis constraint means that any change in timing, background, or color after the fact may require\nrecreating or re-rendering the entire sequences, which can be both time-consuming and costly.\nThat’s why, rigorous planning during pre-production (storyboarding, character design, and \nscene breakdown) is very essential to minimize later revisions.\n4. Conclusion\nThe animation production pipeline is not only a sequence of technical operations; it is a delicate \nbalance between the human artistic intention, the team organization, and the technological \nconstraint. Its efficiency depends as much on the clarity of human creative vision as on the \nprecision of the production management. Understanding its cost, roles, and limits helps future \ncreators appreciate that every animated image is the result of both imagination and discipline, \nfreedom and structure."};
window.ATELIER_COURS["1_yNth1ynLynXkjvN4oe2uUl43zIfpjyz"] = {"title":"Visual Modality","body":"Available on zenodo – DOI : 10.5281/zenodo.17251328\nMemorandum on Animated Multimedia Application Design\nVisual Modality\nAuthor: Abdelkrim Mebarki\nMaster’s Program in Computer Science – Artificial Intelligence & Applications\nDepartment of Computer Science – Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science\nUniversity of Science and Technology of Oran\nOctober 2, 2025\nVisual modality includes different media types: Text, static and dynamic images and 3D graphics.\nPart One – Text modality\nText is a set of symbols that, when combined according to grammatical rules, convey meaning.\n1. Communication Dimension\nIn the context of human–machine interaction, communication through text is straightforward: it \ninvolves the correspondence between numeric data (codes) and symbols.\n2. Interpretation\nIt functions as a symbolic communication system, (~ sign language). Each symbol carries a \nsemantic value, which allows meaning to emerge from structured combinations.\n3. Utility\nWithin multimedia applications, text is often integrated into scenes to:\n➔ Provide additional information and details;\n➔ Clarify or translate conversations;\n➔ Describe contexts and situations.\n4. Implementation\nText is the simplest communication channel and the most accessible perceptual dimension. \nIt offers advantages such as: low storage cost, efficient encoding, and ease of manipulation.\n5. Advanced Tools\nAdvanced text processing relies on computational tools such as:\n➔ Lexical analyzers;\n➔ Syntax analyzers;\n➔ Semantic parsers;\n➔ Generative AI Tools\nPart Two – Vision\n1. Definition\nVision in multimedia refers to the digital representation of visual information, allowing users to \nperceive and interpret images or graphics through the sense of sight.\n2. Digital Representation (Computer Model)\nIn pixel-based model (Raster/Bitmap), each pixel is associated with a value in a model, defined by:\n➔ Depth (Bit Depth) – Number of bits used to represent each pixel (8-bit grayscale, 24-bit RGB).\n➔ Color definition – Grayscale, RGB, CMYK, or other color models.\n➔ Image Resolution – The number of pixels sampled; higher resolution → greater detail.\n➔ Render (display) definition – Maximum number of pixels displayed by a device.\nVector images are defined by mathematical descriptions of shapes, lines, and curves, allowing \ninfinite scalability without loss of quality.\nA color model is a mathematical and conceptual framework used to represent colors as ordered \nsets of numerical values: Each color is encoded using one or more components (e.g., red, green, \nand blue), allowing precise storage, manipulation, and reproduction of visual information.\n\n3. Vector images\nVector images are defined mathematically using algorithms and primitives (points, lines, \ncurves, polygons, transformations). They are resolution-independent because the description can \nbe scaled infinitely without quality loss.\nScreens, however, are made of physical pixels. No matter the original format, the display device \ncan only show bitmaps (a rasterized grid of pixels).\nWhen a vector image is displayed on screen, it undergoes a process called rasterization: the mathematical \ndescription is converted into a bitmap adapted to the screen resolution.\nPrinting devices work similarly: even if you send vector graphics to the printer, they are rasterized internally \naccording to the printer’s DPI.\n3.1. How is vector image information actually encoded in a file?\nUnlike bitmap images, which are stored pixel by pixel, a vector image is stored as mathematical \ninstructions describing shapes, colors, and the arrangement of elements.\n1. Graphic Primitives (the “building blocks”)\n➔ Points (x, y coordinates);\n➔ Simplices (Vertices, Edges, Facets and Faces);\n➔ Curves and Surfaces (Bezier curves, splines, arcs);\n➔ Text (vector fonts, where each character is a shape).\n2. Visual Attributes:\nStroke color, Stroke width (line thickness), Fill color, Gradients, patterns, transparency…\n3. Geometric Transformations:\nTranslation (move), Rotation, Scaling (zoom in/out)…\n4. Hierarchical structure:\nObjects are organized into layers or groups.\n4. Types of Visual Media\nStill images – Static visual representations, such as photos or graphics.\nMoving images (animated) – Dynamic sequences: Videos, Computer Animations…\n5. Utility\nVisual media provide rich information and context within multimedia applications, including:\n➔ Representation of real or simulated environments;\n➔ Communication of abstract concepts through diagrams and illustrations;\n➔ Enhancement of user engagement through animations and visual storytelling.\n6. Implementation\nVision is one of the most data-intensive communication channels. Storage and processing \nrequirements depend on image type:\n➔ Raster images require significant storage and compression (JPEG, PNG).\n➔ Vector images require less storage and allow easy transformations.\n➔ Video and animations involve continuous image sequences, demanding high bandwidth \nand efficient compression (MPEG, MP4, AVI).\n7. Advanced Tools\nWorking with visual media involves a variety of techniques and tools, such as:\n➔ Image Editing (Photoshop, GIMP).\n➔ Vector Design (Illustrator, Inkscape).\n➔ Video Editing and post-production (Premiere, After Effects).\n➔ Rendering engines and animation software (Blender, Unity)."};
window.ATELIER_COURS["14r3PkA2BPUy03JKEnRV7siuO2JCRLEWO"] = {"title":"X3D","body":"University of Science and Technology of Oran – Mohamed Boudiaf (USTOMB) \nFaculty of Mathematics and Informatics\nComputer Science Department\nMultimedia Application & \nVirtual Reality\nX3D\nMaster’s Degree Courses\nArtificial Intelligence and ApplicationsWhat is X3D?\nX3D = Extensible 3D, the modern ISO standard for interactive \ngraphics\nSuccessor of VRML, designed for the modern web\nBuilt on a scene graph with nodes, fields, and events\nUsed in VR, AR, CAD, simulation, scientific visualization\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Scope of X3D\nWeb-based 3D graphics\nInteractive VR/AR environments\nCAD and engineering\nEducation, medicine, and simulations\nSupports XML, Classic VRML, and JSON syntaxes\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)X3D Architecture\nNodes as individual components\nROUTEs connecting events\nSensors for user interaction\nInterpolators for animation\nScripts for logic\nAppearance, textures, and shaders\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Node Types\nGrouping nodes (Transform, Group)\nGeometry (Box, Sphere, IndexedFaceSet)\nAppearance (Material, ImageTexture)\nSensors (TouchSensor, PlaneSensor)\nAnimation (TimeSensor, Interpolators)\nLights (PointLight, DirectionalLight)\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Encodings of X3D\nXML format\nClassic VRML format\nJSON format\nAll represent the same scene graph\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Basic X3D Scene Structure (Concept)\nAn X3D scene always contains:\nThe `<X3D>` root\nA `<Scene>` element\nShapes, transforms, sensors…\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Basic X3D Scene Structure Example\n<X3D>\n<Scene>\n<Shape>\n<Appearance>\n<Material diffuseColor='0 0 1' />\n</Appearance>\n<Box />\n</Shape>\n</Scene>\n</X3D>\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Shapes (Concept)\nBox\nSphere\nCylinder\nCone\nIndexedFaceSet for custom geometry\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Shapes Example\n<Scene>\n<Shape>\n<Appearance>\n<Material diffuseColor='1 0 0'/>\n</Appearance>\n<Sphere />\n</Shape>\n</Scene>\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Appearance & Material (Concept)\nMaterial controls object color\nDefines ambient, diffuse, and specular light behavior\nShininess affects highlights\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Appearance & Material Example\n<Shape>\n<Appearance>\n<Material diffuseColor='0 1 0' shininess='0.8' />\n</Appearance>\n<Box />\n</Shape>\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Transformations (Concept)\ntranslation\nrotation\nscale\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Transformations (Executable Example)\n<Transform translation='0 1.5 0' rotation='0 1 0 1.57'>\n<Shape>\n<Cylinder />\n</Shape>\n</Transform>\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Sensors (Concept)\nTouchSensor detects clicks\nPlaneSensor detects dragging\nCylinderSensor detects rotation\nProximitySensor detects viewer location\nVisibilitySensor detects visibility\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)TouchSensor Example\n<Shape DEF='OBJ'>\n<Appearance>\n<Material diffuseColor='0 0 1' />\n</Appearance>\n<Box />\n</Shape>\n<TouchSensor DEF='CLICKER' />\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Animation & TimeSensor (Concept)\nTimeSensor generates continuous time\nInterpolators convert time into movement\nROUTEs connect animation nodes\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Simple Animation Example\n<Transform DEF='MOVE_ME'>\n<Shape>\n<Box />\n</Shape>\n</Transform>\n<TimeSensor DEF='CLOCK' cycleInterval='3' loop='true' />\n<PositionInterpolator DEF='PATH'\nkey='0 0.5 1'\nkeyValue='0 0 0 0 2 0 0 0 0' />\n<ROUTE fromNode='CLOCK' fromField='fraction_changed' toNode='PATH' \ntoField='set_fraction' />\n<ROUTE fromNode='PATH' fromField='value_changed' toNode='MOVE_ME' \ntoField='set_translation' />\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Sensor & Animation on Cube\nTouchSensor triggers animation\nTimeSensor controls timing\nInterpolator moves the cube\nROUTEs connect all components\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Sensor + Animation Example\n<Scene>\n<Transform DEF='CUBE'>\n<Shape>\n<Appearance>\n<Material diffuseColor='0.2 0.7 1' />\n</Appearance>\n<Box />\n</Shape>\n</Transform>\n<TouchSensor DEF='CLICK' />\n<TimeSensor DEF='TIMER' cycleInterval='2' loop='false' />\n<PositionInterpolator DEF='ANIM'\nkey='0 1'\nkeyValue='0 0 0 0 3 0' />\n<ROUTE fromNode='CLICK' fromField='touchTime' toNode='TIMER' toField='startTime' />\n<ROUTE fromNode='TIMER' fromField='fraction_changed' toNode='ANIM' toField='set_fraction' />\n<ROUTE fromNode='ANIM' fromField='value_changed' toNode='CUBE' toField='set_translation' />\n</Scene>\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)X3D in HTML (Concept)\nX3D can be embedded directly in web pages\nWorks with X3DOM or X_ITE\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)HTML Integration Example\n<x3d width='500px' height='400px'>\n<scene>\n<shape>\n<box></box>\n</shape>\n</scene>\n</x3d>\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)X3D for VR/AR\nCompatible with WebXR\nSupports device sensors\nViewpoints enable head-tracking\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)Summary\nModern 3D standard\nNode-based architecture\nSensors for interaction\nAnimation via TimeSensor and Interpolators\nExtensible with scripts\nReady for Web, VR, and AR\nMultimedia Applications & Virtual Reality (A. Mebarki, 2025)"};
